{"type":"message","id":"2edc7674","parentId":"93690e39","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:25:49.843Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"现在让我阅读关键源文件来理解当前实现，特别是 metisCutter.cpp/h 和 mpiAdapter.cpp/h，这些应该包含 distribute() 和 Interface 相关代码。","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-c41c9ab653fe4ab9b346afb3f09a74f7","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/metisCutter.cpp"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-e0f0a7efee544f92a0bfa18b37feafff","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/metisCutter.h"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-831da63829494f1fb9ac9e4964d98c36","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/mpiAdapter.cpp"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-cab63d49df814741be97fd7e5c65c37e","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/mpiAdapter.h"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":933,"output":168,"cacheRead":12863,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":13964,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1772619945716}}
{"type":"message","id":"ea8defd1","parentId":"2edc7674","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:25:49.850Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-c41c9ab653fe4ab9b346afb3f09a74f7","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#include <algorithm>\n#include <cmath>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <numeric>\n\n#include <cgns_io.h>\n#include <parmetis.h>\n\n#include \"cmdLine.h\"\n#include \"metisCutter.h\"\n#include \"mpiAdapter.h\"\n#include \"stringUtil.h\"\n\nstatic const int DataTAG = 1000;\n\nstatic std::vector<idx_t> distribute(const idx_t len, const int n, idx_t &nCellThisRank)\n{\n    vector<idx_t> dist(n + 1, 0);\n    idx_t base = len / n;\n    idx_t remainder = len % n;\n    int rank;\n    MPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &rank);\n    nCellThisRank = base + (rank < remainder ? 1 : 0);\n    MPI_Allgather(&nCellThisRank, 1, GetMPIDataType<idx_t>(), dist.data() + 1, 1, GetMPIDataType<idx_t>(), MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n    for (int iProc = 1; iProc < n + 1; ++iProc) { dist[iProc] += dist[iProc - 1]; }\n    return dist;\n}\n\nnamespace MeshCut\n{\nMetisCutter::MetisCutter() : bigMesh_(nullptr) {}\n\nMetisCutter::~MetisCutter() {}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::cut(int argc, char **argv)\n{\n    CmdLine cl{};\n    cl.regist<std::string, CmdLine::MustOffer>(\"m\", \"mesh\", \"mesh file name.\", \" \");\n    cl.regist<size_t, CmdLine::MustOffer>(\"np\", \"npart\", \"part number of sub-mesh.\", \" \");\n    cl.parse(argc, argv);\n\n    MPIAdapter::Initialize(&argc, &argv);\n#ifdef DEBUG_MODE\n    if (!MPIAdapter::isParallel() || (MPIAdapter::isParallel() && MPIAdapter::isMaster()))\n    {\n        std::cout << \"DEBUG_MODE: type any char to continue\\n\";\n        auto a = std::getchar();\n    }\n    MPIAdapter::Barrier();\n#endif\n    MPIAdapter::isParallel() ? this->cut_parmetis(cl.get<std::string>(\"mesh\"), cl.get<size_t>(\"npart\")) : this->cut_metis(cl.get<std::string>(\"mesh\"), cl.get<size_t>(\"npart\"));\n}\n\n// collect interface from all other threads\nvoid MetisCutter::collect_interface()\n{\n    // Pack interface data into a string for sending\n    auto packInterface = [&, this]() -> std::string\n    {\n        std::string sendStr;\n        for (auto ifile : outerFace_)\n        {\n            if (ifile.second.empty()) continue;\n\n            sendStr += (std::to_string(ifile.first) + \":\");\n            for (auto face : ifile.second) { sendStr += (std::to_string(face.first) + \";\"); }\n            sendStr += \"#\";\n        }\n\n        return sendStr;\n    };\n\n    // Unpack received interface data\n    auto unpackInterface = [&, this](const std::string &recvStr)\n    {\n        for (auto ifile : stringSplit(recvStr, \"#\"))\n        {\n            if (ifile.empty()) continue;\n            auto cur = stringSplit(ifile, \":\");\n            if (cur.size() < 2) continue;\n            auto nbrFile = std::stoi(cur[0]);\n            if (ownerFile_.count(nbrFile) != 0) continue;\n\n            check(true, \" \", format(\"  Thread %d: recv %d interface of file %d\\n\", RANK, stringSplit(cur[1], \";\").size(), nbrFile));\n\n            auto &nbrOuterface = outerFace_[nbrFile];\n            for (auto faceHash : stringSplit(cur[1], \";\")) { \n                if (!faceHash.empty()) {\n                    nbrOuterface.insert({std::stoull(faceHash), {}});\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    };\n\n    MPIAdapter::Barrier();\n\n    std::string sendTmp = packInterface();\n    int sendCnt = sendTmp.size();\n\n    // Step 1: Determine which ranks have data to send (non-empty send buffer)\n    std::vector<int> sendCnts(SIZE, 0);\n    sendCnts[RANK] = sendCnt;\n    MPI_Allgather(&sendCnt, 1, MPI_INT, sendCnts.data(), 1, MPI_INT, MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n\n    // Step 2: Identify target ranks (those with non-empty data)\n    std::vector<int> targetRanks;\n    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; ++i) {\n        if (i != RANK && sendCnts[i] > 0) {\n            targetRanks.push_back(i);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Step 3: Use point-to-point communication only with target ranks\n    std::vector<MPI_Request> requests;\n    std::vector<int> recvCnt(SIZE, 0);\n    std::vector<std::string> recvBuffers(SIZE);\n    std::vector<char*> recvBuffersPtr(SIZE, nullptr);\n\n    // Post non-blocking receives from all ranks that have data\n    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; ++i) {\n        if (i != RANK && sendCnts[i] > 0) {\n            recvCnt[i] = sendCnts[i];\n            recvBuffers[i].assign(recvCnt[i] + 1, '\\0');\n            recvBuffersPtr[i] = const_cast<char*>(recvBuffers[i].data());\n            requests.push_back(MPI_Request{});\n            MPI_Irecv(recvBuffersPtr[i], recvCnt[i], MPI_CHAR, i, DataTAG + i, MPI_COMM_WORLD, &requests.back());\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Post non-blocking sends to all ranks that have data\n    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; ++i) {\n        if (i != RANK && sendCnts[RANK] > 0) {\n            requests.push_back(MPI_Request{});\n            MPI_Isend(const_cast<char*>(sendTmp.data()), sendCnt, MPI_CHAR, i, RANK + DataTAG, MPI_COMM_WORLD, &requests.back());\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Wait for all communications to complete\n    if (!requests.empty()) {\n        std::vector<MPI_Status> statuses(requests.size());\n        MPIAdapter::WaitAll(requests.size(), requests.data(), statuses.data());\n    }\n\n    MPIAdapter::Barrier();\n\n    // Process received data\n    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; ++i) {\n        if (i != RANK && sendCnts[i] > 0 && !recvBuffers[i].empty()) {\n            unpackInterface(recvBuffers[i]);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// collect cell id from all other threads\nvector<MetisCutter::Cell> MetisCutter::collect_subBody(const MetisCutter::DecomposeResult &decomposeResult, const vector<int> &ownerThread)\n{\n    map<int, vector<Cell>> cellInfo;\n    {\n        for (idx_t i = 0; i < decomposeResult.nCellThisRank; ++i)\n        {\n            int partId = cellPartition_[i];\n            auto threadId = ownerThread[partId];\n            auto cellId = decomposeResult.start + i;\n            cellInfo[threadId].push_back(Cell{cellId, partId});\n        }\n    }\n    vector<idx_t>{}.swap(cellPartition_); // free\n\n    // all process collect send-recv info from all process\n    vector<long> sendInfo(SIZE, 0), recvInfo(SIZE, 0);\n    {\n        for (auto it : cellInfo)\n        {\n            if (it.first != RANK) sendInfo[it.first] += it.second.size();\n        }\n        MPI_Alltoall(sendInfo.data(), 1, MPI_LONG, recvInfo.data(), 1, MPI_LONG, MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n    }\n\n    // send-recv data\n    MPI_Datatype cellType;\n    {\n        const int cnt = 2;\n        int blockLen[cnt] = {1, 1};\n        MPI_Aint disp[cnt] = {0, sizeof(idx_t)};\n        MPI_Datatype types[cnt] = {MPI_LONG, MPI_INT};\n        MPIAdapter::TypeStruct(cnt, blockLen, disp, types, &cellType);\n        MPIAdapter::TypeCommit(&cellType);\n    }\n    vector<MPI_Request> requests;\n    vector<MPI_Status> statuses;\n    vector<vector<Cell>> recvData;\n    // send\n    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; ++i)\n    {\n        if (sendInfo[i] != 0)\n        {\n            requests.push_back(MPI_Request{});\n            MPIAdapter::ISend(cellInfo[i].data(), cellInfo[i].size(), cellType, i, DataTAG + i, MPI_COMM_WORLD, &requests.back());\n        }\n    }\n    // recv\n    for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; ++i)\n    {\n        auto recvDataSize = recvInfo[i];\n        if (recvDataSize != 0)\n        {\n            recvData.push_back(vector<Cell>(recvDataSize, Cell{}));\n            requests.push_back(MPI_Request{});\n            MPI_Irecv(recvData.back().data(), recvDataSize, cellType, i, RANK + DataTAG, MPI_COMM_WORLD, &(requests.back()));\n        }\n    }\n    statuses.assign(requests.size(), MPI_Status{});\n    MPIAdapter::WaitAll(requests.size(), requests.data(), statuses.data());\n    requests.clear(), statuses.clear();\n\n    // update cellInfo\n    for (auto &it : recvData)\n    {\n        for (auto &jt : it) cellInfo[RANK].emplace_back(jt);\n    }\n    MPIAdapter::TypeFree(&cellType);\n\n    return cellInfo[RANK];\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::cut_metis(string meshFilename, const int np)\n{\n    // open mesh file to read/write\n    bigMesh_ = std::make_shared<CGFile>(meshFilename);\n    this->updateOwnerThread(meshFilename, np);\n\n    // load body-data into memory from file\n    auto bigBody = bigMesh_->loadSection(bigMesh_->bodySectionIdList());\n\n    check(true, \"\", format(\"Call METIS_V3_PartMeshKway decompose: %s\\n\", bigBody.name));\n    // cut\n    check(cut_metis(bigBody.data, (idx_t)np, cellPartition_, nodePartition_) == METIS_OK, format(\"Call METIS_V3_PartMeshKway decompose: %s return error %s\", bigBody.name), format(\"Call METIS_V3_PartMeshKway decompose: %s successfuly\\n\", bigBody.name));\n\n    // write sub-mesh data to file\n    for (auto ifile = 0; ifile < np; ++ifile)\n    {\n        // open sub-mesh to write\n        this->openSubMeshToWrite(meshFilename, ifile, np);\n\n        // collect cell/node id of sub-mesh\n        nodeIdG2L_.insert({ifile, {}}); // 1-base id\n        nodeIds_.clear();               // 1-base id\n        cellIds_.clear();               // 0-base id\n        for (auto i = 0; i < cellPartition_.size(); ++i)\n        {\n            if (cellPartition_[i] != ifile) continue;\n\n            cellIds_.insert(i + 1);\n            for (auto it : bigBody.cell(i + 1))\n                if (nodeIds_.count(it) == 0) nodeIds_.insert(it);\n        }\n        for (auto it : nodeIds_) nodeIdG2L_[ifile].insert({it, nodeIdG2L_[ifile].size() + 1});\n\n        // write coordinate\n        this->rwNode(ifile);\n\n        // write body-section\n        this->rwBody(ifile, bigBody);\n\n        // write bdy-section\n        this->rwBoundary(ifile);\n\n        // write interface\n        this->rwInterface(ifile);\n\n        // clear\n        for (auto i = 0; i <= ifile; ++i)\n        {\n            if (outerFace_[i].empty())\n            {\n                nodeIdG2L_[i].clear();\n                smallMesh_[i]->close();\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // clear\n    bigMesh_->close();\n    bigBody.clear();\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::cut_parmetis(string meshFilename, const int np)\n{\n    RANK = MPIAdapter::rank(), SIZE = MPIAdapter::size();\n\n    if (SIZE > np)\n    {\n        if (MPIAdapter::isMaster()) check(true, \"\", format(\"MPI size should not bigger than subMesh number: %d > %d\\n\", SIZE, np));\n\n        MPIAdapter::Finalize();\n        exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n    }\n\n    // open mesh file to read/write\n    bigMesh_ = std::make_shared<CGFile>(meshFilename);\n    this->updateOwnerThread(meshFilename, np);\n\n    // handle bodysection\n    auto bigBody = bigMesh_->bodySection();\n\n    // decompose body-section into np parts,\n    // and collect subBody belong to each process\n    auto comPartId = [](const Cell &lhs, const Cell &rhs) { return lhs.partId < rhs.partId; };\n    auto comId = [](const Cell &lhs, const Cell &rhs) { return lhs.id < rhs.id; };\n    auto subBody = collect_subBody(this->decompose_body(bigBody, np), ownerThread_);\n    if (subBody.size() == 0) std::cout << format(\"Thread %d has no subBody\\n\", RANK);\n    std::sort(subBody.begin(), subBody.end(), comPartId);\n    auto pos = subBody.begin();\n    while (pos != subBody.end())\n    {\n        // relod body-section\n        auto lastPos = pos;\n        const auto ifile = lastPos->partId;\n        pos = std::upper_bound(lastPos, subBody.end(), *lastPos, comPartId);\n        std::sort(lastPos, pos, comId);\n        auto indexLB = lastPos->id, indexUB = (pos - 1)->id;\n        auto curBody = bigMesh_->loadSection(bigMesh_->bodySectionIdList(), indexLB, indexUB);\n        // check(true, \"\", format(\"  Thread %d: load %d cells [%d-%d] of part %d\\n\", RANK, indexUB-indexLB+1, indexLB, indexUB, ifile));\n\n        // open sub-mesh file for writing (CRITICAL: must be done before rwNode/rwBody)\n        this->openSubMeshToWrite(meshFilename, ifile, np);\n\n        // cell and node\n        cellIds_.clear(), nodeIds_.clear();\n        for (auto cell : subBody)\n        {\n            if (cell.partId != ifile) continue;\n            cellIds_.insert(cell.id);\n            for (auto it : curBody.cell(cell.id))\n                if (nodeIds_.count(it) == 0) nodeIds_.insert(it);\n        }\n        nodeIdG2L_.insert({ifile, {}});\n        if (!nodeIds_.empty())\n            for (auto it : nodeIds_) nodeIdG2L_[ifile].insert({it, nodeIdG2L_[ifile].size() + 1});\n\n        // write coordinate\n        this->rwNode(ifile);\n\n        // write body\n        this->rwBody(ifile, curBody);\n\n        // write bdy\n        this->rwBoundary(ifile);\n    }\n    bigBody.clear();\n\n    // collect interface\n    this->collect_interface();\n\n    // write interface\n    for (auto ifile : ownerFile_) this->writeInterface(ifile, np);\n\n    // clear\n    bigMesh_->close();\n    for (auto it : smallMesh_)\n        if (it != nullptr) it->close();\n\n    MPIAdapter::Barrier();\n}\n\nint MetisCutter::cut_metis(const vector<vector<idx_t>> &cellToplogy, idx_t np, vector<idx_t> &cellPartition, vector<idx_t> &nodePartition)\n{\n    // metis options\n    idx_t options[METIS_NOPTIONS];\n    options[METIS_OPTION_PTYPE] = METIS_PTYPE_KWAY;\n    options[METIS_OPTION_OBJTYPE] = METIS_OBJTYPE_CUT;\n    options[METIS_OPTION_NUMBERING] = 0;\n    // options[METIS_OPTION_CTYPE] = METIS_CTYPE_SHEM;\n    // options[METIS_OPTION_IPTYPE] = METIS_IPTYPE_EDGE;\n    // options[METIS_OPTION_RTYPE] = METIS_RTYPE_GREEDY;\n    // options[METIS_OPTION_NCUTS] = 1;\n    // options[METIS_OPTION_NITER] = 10;\n    // options[METIS_OPTION_UFACTOR] = 30;\n    METIS_SetDefaultOptions(options);\n\n    // eind-eptr\n    std::vector<idx_t> eind, eptr(cellToplogy.size() + 1, 0);\n    cgsize_t offset = 0;\n    for (auto i = 0; i < cellToplogy.size(); ++i)\n    {\n        for (auto jt : cellToplogy[i]) { eind.push_back(jt - 1); }\n        offset += cellToplogy[i].size();\n        eptr[i + 1] = offset;\n    }\n    idx_t nCell = cellToplogy.size();\n    idx_t nNode = bigMesh_->nNode();\n    cellPartition.assign(nCell, 0);\n    nodePartition.assign(nNode, 0);\n    idx_t ncommon = 4;\n    idx_t objval;\n\n    auto rst = METIS_PartMeshDual(&nCell, &nNode, eptr.data(), eind.data(), NULL, NULL, &ncommon, &np, NULL, options, &objval, cellPartition.data(), nodePartition.data());\n\n    nodePartition.clear();\n    vector<idx_t>{}.swap(nodePartition);\n\n    return rst;\n}\n\nint MetisCutter::cut_parmetis(idx_t np, vector<idx_t> &elmdist, vector<idx_t> &eptr, vector<idx_t> &eind, vector<idx_t> &cellPartition)\n{\n    // parallel read element connectivity data\n    idx_t wgtflag = 0;      // 0: no weights(elmwgt is null), 2: weights on the vertices only\n    idx_t numflag = 0;      // 0: C-style numbering, 1: Fortran-style numbering\n    idx_t ncon = 1;         // the number of weights that each vertex has.\n    idx_t ncommonnodes = 2; // should be greater than 0, 2 is ok for most meshes.\n    idx_t edgecut = 0;\n    idx_t opts[3] = {0, 1, static_cast<idx_t>(std::time(nullptr))};\n    real_t tpwgts[ncon * np];\n    real_t ubvec[ncon];\n\n    for (auto i = 0; i < ncon * np; ++i) { tpwgts[i] = 1.0 / (real_t)np; }\n    for (auto i = 0; i < ncon; ++i) { ubvec[i] = 1.05; }\n\n    MPI_Comm mpi_comm = MPI_COMM_WORLD;\n    return ParMETIS_V3_PartMeshKway(elmdist.data(), eptr.data(), eind.data(),\n                                    NULL, // weights of the elements\n                                    &wgtflag, &numflag, &ncon, &ncommonnodes, &np, tpwgts, ubvec, opts, &edgecut, cellPartition.data(), &mpi_comm);\n}\n\nMetisCutter::DecomposeResult MetisCutter::decompose_body(const CGFile::Section &bigBody, const int np)\n{\n    DecomposeResult result;\n\n    auto elmdist = distribute(bigBody.end - bigBody.start + 1, SIZE, result.nCellThisRank);\n    result.start = bigBody.start + elmdist[RANK];\n\n    auto shareBigbody = bigMesh_->loadSection(bigMesh_->bodySectionIdList(), result.start, result.start + result.nCellThisRank - 1);\n\n    std::vector<idx_t> eind, eptr{0};\n    idx_t beg = shareBigbody.isMixed() ? 1 : 0;\n    for (auto id = result.start; id < result.start + result.nCellThisRank; ++id)\n    {\n        auto &tmp = shareBigbody.cell(id);\n        for (int j = beg; j < tmp.size(); ++j) { eind.push_back(tmp[j] - 1); }\n        eptr.push_back(eptr.back() + tmp.size() - beg);\n    }\n    check(true, \"\", format(\"ParMETIS_V3_PartMeshKway begin divide: %s\\n\", shareBigbody.name));\n    cellPartition_.assign(result.nCellThisRank, 0);\n    check(cut_parmetis(np, elmdist, eptr, eind, cellPartition_) == METIS_OK, format(\"Process %d: ParMETIS_V3_PartMeshKway return error while divide section: %s\\n\", RANK, bigBody.name), format(\"ParMETIS_V3_PartMeshKway done\\n\"));\n\n    return result;\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::updateOwnerThread(string bigFileName, const int np)\n{\n    idx_t dummy;\n\n    // calculate partId collection of each thread\n    ownerThread_.assign(np, 0);\n    {\n        auto tmp = distribute(np, SIZE, dummy);\n        for (auto i = 1; i <= SIZE; ++i)\n        {\n            for (auto j = tmp[i - 1]; j < tmp[i]; ++j) ownerThread_[j] = i - 1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    smallMesh_.assign(np, nullptr);\n    // for(auto i=0; i<np; ++i)\n    // {\n    //     if(ownerThread[i] == RANK)\n    //     {\n    //         smallMesh_[i] = std::make_shared<CGFile>(smallMeshName(bigFileName, i, np), CG_MODE_WRITE);\n    //         ownerFile_.insert(i);\n    //     }\n    // }\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::openSubMeshToWrite(string filename, const int id, const int np)\n{\n    if (ownerThread_[id] == RANK)\n    {\n        smallMesh_[id] = std::make_shared<CGFile>(smallMeshName(filename, id, np), CG_MODE_WRITE);\n        ownerFile_.insert(id);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::rwBody(const int ifile, const CGFile::Section &bigBody)\n{\n    auto &subFile = smallMesh_[ifile];\n\n    // new a sectionk\n    auto &curS = subFile->addSection();\n    strcpy(curS.name, bigBody.name);\n    curS.cellType = bigBody.cellType;\n    for (auto id : cellIds_) { curS.addCell(id, bigBody.cell(id), bigBody.flag(id)); }\n\n    // write data\n    subFile->writeSection(curS, nodeIdG2L_[ifile]);\n\n    // global info\n    auto len = curS.end - curS.start + 1;\n    subFile->writeGlobalInfo(curS, bigBody.data.size(), globalOffset_, globalOffset_ + len);\n    globalOffset_ += len;\n\n    // update outerface\n    this->updateOuterFace(curS, ifile);\n\n    // clear\n    curS.clear();\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::rwBoundary(const int ifile)\n{\n    auto &subFile = smallMesh_[ifile];\n    auto &curOuterFace = outerFace_[ifile];\n\n    for (auto ithSection : bigMesh_->bdySectionIdList())\n    {\n        auto &subBdy = subFile->addSection();\n        auto &bigBdy = bigMesh_->loadSection(ithSection);\n        strcpy(subBdy.name, bigBdy.name);\n        subBdy.cellType = bigBdy.cellType;\n\n        cgsize_t ID = bigBdy.start;\n        for (auto i = bigBdy.start; i <= bigBdy.end; ++i)\n        {\n            auto face = bigBdy.cell(i);\n            auto h = hashFace(face);\n            if (curOuterFace.find(h) != curOuterFace.end())\n            {\n                curOuterFace.erase(h);\n                subBdy.addCell(ID++, face, bigBdy.flag(i));\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (!subBdy.data.empty()) subFile->writeSection(subBdy, nodeIdG2L_[ifile]);\n\n        // clear\n        subBdy.clear();\n    }\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::rwInterface(const int id)\n{\n    auto &subFile = smallMesh_[id];\n    auto &curOuterFace = outerFace_[id];\n\n    for (auto nbr = 0; nbr < id; ++nbr)\n    {\n        if (outerFace_[nbr].empty()) continue;\n\n        auto &nbrOuterFace = outerFace_[nbr];\n\n        set<cgsize_t> typeFlags;\n        CGFile::Section curS, nbrS;\n        for (auto face = nbrOuterFace.begin(); face != nbrOuterFace.end();)\n        {\n            auto it = curOuterFace.find(face->first);\n            if (it != curOuterFace.end())\n            {\n                curS.data.emplace_back(it->second);\n                nbrS.data.push_back(face->second);\n\n                auto t = CGFile::CellType(it->second.size(), 2);\n                curS.typeFlag.push_back(t);\n                nbrS.typeFlag.push_back(t);\n\n                typeFlags.insert(it->second.size());\n\n                it = curOuterFace.erase(it);\n                face = nbrOuterFace.erase(face);\n            }\n            else\n            {\n                ++face;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!curS.data.empty())\n        {\n            strcpy(curS.name, format(\"Interface_%d\", nbr).c_str());\n            strcpy(nbrS.name, format(\"Interface_%d\", id).c_str());\n\n            if (typeFlags.size() > 1)\n            {\n                curS.cellType = ElementType_t::MIXED;\n                nbrS.cellType = ElementType_t::MIXED;\n                auto curOffset = 0;\n                for (auto it : curS.data)\n                {\n                    curS.offset.push_back(curOffset);\n                    nbrS.offset.push_back(curOffset);\n                    curOffset += it.size() + 1;\n                }\n            }\n            else if (typeFlags.size() == 1)\n            {\n                auto type = (*typeFlags.begin() == 3) ? ElementType_t::TRI_3 : ElementType_t::QUAD_4;\n                curS.cellType = type;\n                nbrS.cellType = type;\n            }\n            subFile->writeSection(curS, nodeIdG2L_[id]);\n            smallMesh_[nbr]->writeSection(nbrS, nodeIdG2L_[nbr]);\n        }\n\n        // clear\n        vector<vector<cgsize_t>>{}.swap(curS.data);\n        vector<cgsize_t>{}.swap(curS.offset);\n        vector<cgsize_t>{}.swap(curS.typeFlag);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::rwNode(const int ifile)\n{\n    auto &subFile = smallMesh_[ifile];\n\n    // zone info\n    subFile->nCell(cellIds_.size());\n    subFile->nNode(nodeIds_.size());\n\n    // node data\n    auto dataTmp = bigMesh_->loadCoordinate(*nodeIds_.begin(), *nodeIds_.rbegin());\n    vector<vector<double>> data(3, vector<double>(nodeIds_.size(), 0.0));\n    auto start = *nodeIds_.begin();\n    cgsize_t i = 0;\n    for (auto id : nodeIds_)\n    {\n        auto loc = id - start;\n        data[0][i] = dataTmp[0][loc];\n        data[1][i] = dataTmp[1][loc];\n        data[2][i] = dataTmp[2][loc];\n        ++i;\n    }\n    smallMesh_[ifile]->writeCoordinate(data);\n}\n\nstd::string MetisCutter::smallMeshName(string bigMeshName, const int id, const int np)\n{\n    char fmt[128], smallFilename[128];\n    // sprintf(fmt, \"%%s.%d.%%0%dd.cgns\", np, int(std::log10(np) + 1));\n    // sprintf(smallFilename, fmt, bigMeshName.substr(0, bigMeshName.size() - 5).c_str(), id);\n    sprintf(fmt, \"decomposed_mesh_%%0%dd.cgns\", int(std::log10(np) + 1));\n    sprintf(smallFilename, fmt, id);\n\n    return smallFilename;\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::updateOuterFace(const CGFile::Section &curS, const int ifile)\n{\n    auto &curOuterFace = outerFace_[ifile];\n\n    for (auto i = 0; i < curS.data.size(); ++i)\n    {\n        auto cellType = curS.isMixed() ? CGFile::CellType(curS.typeFlag[i]) : curS.cellType;\n        for (auto it : CGFile::allFaceInCell(curS.data[i], cellType))\n        {\n            auto h = hashFace(it);\n            if (curOuterFace.find(h) != curOuterFace.end()) { curOuterFace.erase(h); }\n            else\n            {\n                curOuterFace.insert({h, it});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::writeGlobalInfo(const CGFile::Section &curS, const int id)\n{\n    auto len = curS.end - curS.start + 1;\n    smallMesh_[id]->writeGlobalInfo(curS, 10, globalOffset_, globalOffset_ + len);\n    globalOffset_ += len;\n}\n\nvoid MetisCutter::writeInterface(const int id, const int np)\n{\n    auto &subFile = smallMesh_[id];\n    auto &curOuterFace = outerFace_[id];\n\n    for (auto nbr = 0; nbr < np; ++nbr)\n    {\n        if (nbr == id) continue;\n        auto nbrIsOwner = ownerFile_.count(nbr) != 0;\n\n        auto &nbrOuterFace = outerFace_[nbr];\n\n        set<cgsize_t> typeFlags;\n        CGFile::Section curS, nbrS;\n        for (auto face = nbrOuterFace.begin(); face != nbrOuterFace.end();)\n        {\n            auto it = curOuterFace.find(face->first);\n            if (it != curOuterFace.end())\n            {\n                curS.data.emplace_back(it->second);\n                auto t = CGFile::CellType(it->second.size(), 2);\n                curS.typeFlag.push_back(t);\n                typeFlags.insert(it->second.size());\n                it = curOuterFace.erase(it);\n\n                if (nbrIsOwner)\n                {\n                    nbrS.data.push_back(face->second);\n                    nbrS.typeFlag.push_back(t);\n                    face = nbrOuterFace.erase(face);\n                }\n            }\n            else\n            {\n                ++face;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!curS.data.empty())\n        {\n            strcpy(curS.name, format(\"Interface_%d\", nbr).c_str());\n            if (nbrIsOwner) strcpy(nbrS.name, format(\"Interface_%d\", id).c_str());\n\n            if (typeFlags.size() > 1)\n            {\n                curS.cellType = ElementType_t::MIXED;\n                if (nbrIsOwner) nbrS.cellType = ElementType_t::MIXED;\n            }\n            else if (typeFlags.size() == 1)\n            {\n                auto type = (*typeFlags.begin() == 3) ? ElementType_t::TRI_3 : ElementType_t::QUAD_4;\n                curS.cellType = type;\n                if (nbrIsOwner) nbrS.cellType = type;\n            }\n            subFile->writeSection(curS, nodeIdG2L_[id]);\n            if (nbrIsOwner) smallMesh_[nbr]->writeSection(nbrS, nodeIdG2L_[nbr]);\n        }\n\n        // clear\n        vector<vector<cgsize_t>>{}.swap(curS.data);\n        vector<cgsize_t>{}.swap(curS.offset);\n        vector<cgsize_t>{}.swap(curS.typeFlag);\n    }\n}\n\n} // namespace MeshCut\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619949848}}
{"type":"message","id":"89c57a19","parentId":"ea8defd1","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:25:49.857Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-e0f0a7efee544f92a0bfa18b37feafff","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#pragma once\n\n#include <memory>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <map>\n#include <functional>\n\n#ifdef SUPPORT_METIS\n#include <metis.h>\n#else \n    typedef int64_t idx_t;\n#endif\n\n#include \"meshCutter.h\"\n#include \"cgFile.h\"\n\nusing std::map;\nusing std::unordered_map;\n\n// Hash function for face (vector of cgsize_t)\ninline size_t hashFace(const vector<cgsize_t>& face) {\n    size_t h = 0;\n    for (auto id : face)\n        h ^= std::hash<cgsize_t>{}(id) + 0x9e3779b9 + (h << 6) + (h >> 2);\n    return h;\n}\n\n\nnamespace MeshCut\n{\nclass MetisCutter : public MeshCutter\n{\npublic:\n    MetisCutter();\n    virtual ~MetisCutter();\n\npublic:\n    void cut(int argc, char** argv) override;\n\nprivate:\n    using cgns_filetype = std::shared_ptr<CGFile>;\n    int RANK = 0, SIZE = 1;\n    cgns_filetype bigMesh_;\n    vector<cgns_filetype> smallMesh_;\n    map<int, map<cgsize_t, cgsize_t>> nodeIdG2L_;\n    set<cgsize_t> nodeIds_, cellIds_;\n    vector<idx_t> cellPartition_, nodePartition_;\n    unordered_map<int, unordered_map<size_t, vector<cgsize_t>>> outerFace_;\n    cgsize_t globalOffset_ = 0;\n    set<int> ownerFile_;\n    struct DecomposeResult\n    {\n        idx_t nCellThisRank, start;\n    };\n    struct Cell\n    {\n        idx_t id;   // begin from 1\n        int partId; // begin from 0;\n    };\n    vector<int> ownerThread_;\n\nprivate:\n    void collect_interface();\n    \n    vector<Cell> collect_subBody(const DecomposeResult& decomposeResult, const vector<int>& ownerThread);\n\n    void cut_metis(string mesh, const int np);\n\n    int cut_metis(const vector<vector<idx_t>>& cellToplogy, idx_t np, vector<idx_t>& cellPartition, vector<idx_t>& nodePartition);\n\n    void cut_parmetis(string mesh, const int np);\n\n    int cut_parmetis(idx_t np, vector<idx_t>& elmdist, vector<idx_t>& eptr, vector<idx_t>& eind, vector<idx_t>& cellPartition);\n\n    DecomposeResult decompose_body(const CGFile::Section& bigBody, const int np);\n\n    void updateOwnerThread(string filename, const int np);\n\n    void openSubMeshToWrite(string filename, const int id, const int np);\n\n    void rwBody(const int ifile, const CGFile::Section& bigBody);\n\n    void rwBoundary(const int ifile);\n\n    void rwInterface(const int ifile);\n\n    void rwNode(const int ifile);\n\n    std::string smallMeshName(string bigMeshName, const int id, const int np);\n\n    void updateOuterFace(const CGFile::Section& curS, const int id);\n\n    void writeGlobalInfo(const CGFile::Section& curS, const int id);\n\n    void writeInterface(const int ifile, const int np);\n};\n} // namespace MeshCut\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619949855}}
{"type":"message","id":"b83f4e9f","parentId":"89c57a19","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:25:49.863Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-831da63829494f1fb9ac9e4964d98c36","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#include \"mpiAdapter.h\"\n\nstd::unique_ptr<compi::Environment> MPIAdapter::_env = nullptr;\ncompi::Context* MPIAdapter::_ctx = nullptr;\nint MPIAdapter::_size = 1;\nint MPIAdapter::_rank = 0;\n\nvoid MPIAdapter::Initialize(int *argc, char ***argv)\n{\n    // Initialize MPI via compi::Environment\n    _env = std::make_unique<compi::Environment>(compi::ThreadLevel::Multiple);\n    _ctx = &compi::Context::for_comm(MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n    \n    MPI_Comm_size(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &_size);\n    MPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &_rank);\n}\n\nvoid MPIAdapter::Finalize()\n{\n    _ctx = nullptr;\n    _env.reset();\n    _size = 1;\n    _rank = 0;\n}\n\nvoid MPIAdapter::RequestFree(MPI_Request *request)\n{\n    MPI_Request_free(request);\n}\n\nvoid MPIAdapter::TypeContiguous(const int count, MPI_Datatype oldtype, MPI_Datatype *newType)\n{\n    MPI_Type_contiguous(count, oldtype, newType);\n}\n\nvoid MPIAdapter::TypeStruct(int count, int *blk_len, MPI_Aint *disp, MPI_Datatype *types, MPI_Datatype *newType)\n{\n    MPI_Type_create_struct(count, blk_len, disp, types, newType);\n}\n\nvoid MPIAdapter::TypeCommit(MPI_Datatype *newType)\n{\n    MPI_Type_commit(newType);\n}\n\nvoid MPIAdapter::TypeFree(MPI_Datatype *newType)\n{\n    MPI_Type_free(newType);\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619949861}}
{"type":"message","id":"27ab166d","parentId":"b83f4e9f","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:25:49.868Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-cab63d49df814741be97fd7e5c65c37e","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#pragma once\n\n#include <compi/context.h>\n#include <compi/collectives.h>\n\n#include <mpi.h>\n#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <type_traits>\n#include <memory>\n\ntemplate <typename DOUBLE, typename std::enable_if<std::is_same<DOUBLE, double>::value, int>::type = 0>\nconstexpr MPI_Datatype GetMPIDataType()\n{\n    return MPI_DOUBLE;\n}\n\ntemplate <typename FLOAT, typename std::enable_if<std::is_same<FLOAT, float>::value, int>::type = 0>\nconstexpr MPI_Datatype GetMPIDataType()\n{\n    return MPI_FLOAT;\n}\n\ntemplate <typename INTEGER, typename std::enable_if<std::is_integral<INTEGER>::value, int>::type = 0>\nconstexpr MPI_Datatype GetMPIDataType()\n{\n    return std::is_signed<INTEGER>::value ? (sizeof(INTEGER) == 1 ? MPI_INT8_T : (sizeof(INTEGER) == 2 ? MPI_INT16_T : (sizeof(INTEGER) == 4 ? MPI_INT32_T : (sizeof(INTEGER) == 8 ? MPI_INT64_T : MPI_DATATYPE_NULL))))\n                                          : (sizeof(INTEGER) == 1 ? MPI_UINT8_T : (sizeof(INTEGER) == 2 ? MPI_UINT16_T : (sizeof(INTEGER) == 4 ? MPI_UINT32_T : (sizeof(INTEGER) == 8 ? MPI_UINT64_T : MPI_DATATYPE_NULL))));\n}\n\nclass MPIAdapter\n{\nprivate:\n    static std::unique_ptr<compi::Environment> _env;\n    static compi::Context* _ctx;\n    static int _size;\n    static int _rank;\n\npublic:\n    static void Initialize(int *argc, char ***argv);\n    static void Finalize();\n\n    static inline int size() { return _size; }\n    static inline int rank() { return _rank; }\n\n    static inline bool isMaster() { return _rank == 0; }\n    static inline bool isLast() { return _rank == _size - 1; }\n    static inline bool isParallel() { return _size > 1; }\n\n    static inline void Barrier() { MPI_Barrier(MPI_COMM_WORLD); }\n\n    template <typename DATATYPE>\n    static void Bcast(DATATYPE *buf, int count, int root)\n    {\n        if (isParallel()) {\n            MPI_Bcast(buf, count, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), root, MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n        }\n    }\n\n    template <typename DATATYPE>\n    static void Send(const DATATYPE *data, int count, int dest, int tag)\n    {\n        MPI_Send(data, count, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), dest, tag, MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n    }\n\n    template <typename DATATYPE>\n    static void Recv(DATATYPE *data, int count, int src, int tag, MPI_Status *status)\n    {\n        MPI_Recv(data, count, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), src, tag, MPI_COMM_WORLD, status);\n    }\n\n    template <typename DATATYPE>\n    static void ISend(const DATATYPE *buf, int count, int dest, int tag, MPI_Request *request)\n    {\n        MPI_Isend(buf, count, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), dest, tag, MPI_COMM_WORLD, request);\n    }\n\n    inline static void ISend(const void *buf, int count, MPI_Datatype type, int dest, int tag, MPI_Comm comm, MPI_Request *request)\n    {\n        MPI_Isend(buf, count, type, dest, tag, comm, request);\n    }\n\n    template <typename DATATYPE>\n    static void IRecv(DATATYPE *buf, int count, int src, int tag, MPI_Request *request)\n    {\n        MPI_Irecv(buf, count, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), src, tag, MPI_COMM_WORLD, request);\n    }\n\n    inline static void IRecv(void *buf, int count, MPI_Datatype type, int src, int tag, MPI_Comm comm, MPI_Request *request)\n    {\n        MPI_Irecv(buf, count, type, src, tag, comm, request);\n    }\n\n    template <typename DATATYPE>\n    static void AllReduce(DATATYPE& src, DATATYPE& dst, int count, MPI_Op op)\n    {\n        if (isParallel())\n        {\n            MPI_Allreduce(&src, &dst, count, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), op, MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n        }\n        else{\n            dst = src;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    static void WaitAll(int count, MPI_Request *requests, MPI_Status *statuses)\n    {\n        MPI_Waitall(count, requests, statuses);\n    }\n\n    static void RequestFree(MPI_Request *request);\n\n    template <typename DATATYPE>\n    static void AllGatherV(DATATYPE* sendBuf, int count, std::vector<DATATYPE>& recvBuf, std::vector<int>& disp)\n    {\n        if (!isParallel()) {\n            recvBuf.assign(sendBuf, sendBuf + count);\n            disp.assign(1, count);\n            return;\n        }\n        \n        int *lenAll = new int[MPIAdapter::size()];\n        MPI_Allgather(&count, 1, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), lenAll, 1, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), MPI_COMM_WORLD);      \n        int lenTotal = std::accumulate(lenAll, lenAll+MPIAdapter::size(), 0);\n        recvBuf.assign(lenTotal, 0);\n        disp.assign(MPIAdapter::size(),0);\n        for(int i=1; i<MPIAdapter::size(); ++i)\n        {\n            disp[i] = disp[i-1] + lenAll[i-1];\n        }\n        MPI_Allgatherv(sendBuf, count, GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), recvBuf.data(), lenAll, disp.data(), GetMPIDataType<DATATYPE>(), MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n\n        disp[0] = lenAll[0];\n        for(int i=1; i<MPIAdapter::size(); ++i)\n        {\n            disp[i] = disp[i-1] + lenAll[i]; \n        }\n        delete[] lenAll;\n    }\n\n    static void TypeContiguous(const int count, MPI_Datatype oldtype, MPI_Datatype *newType);\n    static void TypeStruct(int count, int *blk_len, MPI_Aint *disp, MPI_Datatype *types, MPI_Datatype *newType);\n    static void TypeCommit(MPI_Datatype *newType);\n    static void TypeFree(MPI_Datatype *newType);\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619949866}}
{"type":"message","id":"80fe6241","parentId":"27ab166d","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:04.896Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"现在我已经阅读了主要代码文件。让我分析三个问题：\n\n### 问题1: distribute() 负载不均衡\n\n在 `metisCutter.cpp` 中的 `distribute` 函数：\n```cpp\nstatic std::vector<idx_t> distribute(const idx_t len, const int n, idx_t &nCellThisRank)\n{\n    vector<idx_t> dist(n + 1, 0);\n    idx_t base = len / n;\n    idx_t remainder = len % n;\n    int rank;\n    MPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &rank);\n    nCellThisRank = base + (rank < remainder ? 1 : 0);\n    MPI_Allgather(&nCellThisRank, 1, GetMPIDataType<idx_t>(), dist.data() + 1, 1, GetMPIDataType<idx_t>(), MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n    for (int iProc = 1; iProc < n + 1; ++iProc) { dist[iProc] += dist[iProc - 1]; }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n这个函数简单地将数据按进程数均匀分配，但实际上：\n1. ParMETIS 的划分结果可能导致不同进程的工作量差异很大\n2. `ownerThread` 的分配也是简单的线性分配，没有考虑实际负载\n\n### 问题2: Interface 全量广播 O(SIZE²)\n\n在 `collect_interface()` 函数中：\n```cpp\n// 使用点对点通信替代了原来的全量广播\n// 但仍然是 O(SIZE²) 的复杂度 - 每个进程都要和所有其他进程通信\n\n\n```\n\n我注意到通信策略需要优化。当前的实现会导致通信开销随进程数平方增长，这对大规模并行计算是不利的。需要设计一种更高效的通信模式，减少不必要的进程间通信。\n\n对于字符串匹配，我建议采用哈希表查找方法，这将显著提升查找性能。通过使用哈希映射，可以将查找时间复杂度从线性降低到接近常数级别。","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"现在我需要查看更多代码来理解 Interface 处理和字符串匹配的具体实现："},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-b8e66c22d7ab427d9961c24bfe038df3","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/stringUtil.h"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-7d1d97c0f14149d6b6451cb01b55afb6","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/stringUtil.cpp"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-2b13dcddc9524fcb8792721927ae92a3","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/cgFile.h"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":23140,"output":915,"cacheRead":51,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":24106,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1772619949869}}
{"type":"message","id":"f84c2a73","parentId":"80fe6241","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:04.920Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-b8e66c22d7ab427d9961c24bfe038df3","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#ifndef STRING_UTIL_H\n#define STRING_UTIL_H\n\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nusing std::string;\nusing std::vector;\n\nstd::string stringJoin(const std::vector<std::string> &strs, std::string &&connector);\nstd::vector<std::string> stringSplit(const std::string &str, const std::string &delimiter);\nstd::vector<std::string> stringSplit(std::string &&str, const std::string &delimiter);\nstd::string stringToLower(const string &str);\nstd::string stringToLower(string &&str);\nstd::string stringTrimLeft(string str);\nstd::string stringTrimRight(string str);\nstd::string stringTrim(string str);\nbool stringRegexFind(string rule, string str);\n\n#endif"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619964915}}
{"type":"message","id":"8b2b38e1","parentId":"f84c2a73","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:04.930Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-7d1d97c0f14149d6b6451cb01b55afb6","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#include <algorithm>\n#include <regex>\n\n#include \"stringUtil.h\"\n\n\n#include <regex>\n\n#if __cplusplus >= 201103L &&                             \\\n    (!defined(__GLIBCXX__) || (__cplusplus >= 201402L) || \\\n        (defined(_GLIBCXX_REGEX_DFS_QUANTIFIERS_LIMIT) || \\\n         defined(_GLIBCXX_REGEX_STATE_LIMIT)           || \\\n             (defined(_GLIBCXX_RELEASE)                && \\\n             _GLIBCXX_RELEASE > 4)))\n#define HAVE_WORKING_REGEX 1\n#else\n#define HAVE_WORKING_REGEX 0\n#endif\n\nconst std::string WHITESPACE=\" \\n\\r\\t\\f\\v\";\n\nstd::string stringJoin(const std::vector<std::string>& strs, std::string&& connector)\n{\n    std::string rst;\n    if(strs.empty()) return rst;\n    rst += strs[0];\n    for(auto i=1; i<strs.size(); ++i)\n    {\n        rst += (connector+strs[i]);\n    }\n    return rst;\n};\n\nstd::vector<std::string> stringSplit(const std::string& str, const std::string& delimiter)\n{\n    std::vector<std::string> rst;\n    std::string::size_type beg = 0, end = 0;\n\n    while ((end = str.find(delimiter, beg)) != std::string::npos)\n    {\n        // avoid empty element (caused by doubled slashes)\n        if (beg < end) { rst.push_back(str.substr(beg, end - beg)); }\n        beg = end + 1;\n    }\n\n    // avoid empty trailing element\n    if (beg < str.size()) { rst.push_back(str.substr(beg, std::string::npos)); }\n\n    return rst;\n}\n\nstd::vector<std::string> stringSplit(std::string&& str, const std::string& delimiter)\n{\n    std::vector<std::string> rst;\n    std::string::size_type beg = 0, end = 0;\n\n    while ((end = str.find(delimiter, beg)) != std::string::npos)\n    {\n        // avoid empty element (caused by doubled slashes)\n        if (beg < end) { rst.push_back(str.substr(beg, end - beg)); }\n        beg = end + 1;\n    }\n\n    // avoid empty trailing element\n    if (beg < str.size()) { rst.push_back(str.substr(beg, std::string::npos)); }\n\n    return rst;\n}\n\n\nstd::string stringToLower(const string& str)\n{\n    string rst;\n    std::transform(str.begin(), str.end(), std::back_inserter(rst), [](char c) -> char{ return std::tolower(c); });\n    \n    return rst;\n}\n\nstd::string stringToLower(string&& str)\n{\n    string rst;\n    std::transform(str.begin(), str.end(), std::back_inserter(rst), [](char c) -> char{ return std::tolower(c); });\n    \n    return rst;\n}\n\nstd::string stringTrimLeft(string str)\n{\n    auto pos = str.find_first_not_of(WHITESPACE);\n    return (pos==std::string::npos) ? \"\" : str.substr(pos);\n}\n\nstd::string stringTrimRight(string str)\n{\n    auto pos = str.find_last_not_of(WHITESPACE);\n    return (pos==std::string::npos) ? \"\" : str.substr(0, pos+1);\n}\n\nstd::string stringTrim(string str)\n{\n    return stringTrimLeft(stringTrimRight(str));\n}\n\nbool stringRegexFind(string rule, string str)\n{\n#if HAVE_WORKING_REGEX\n    std::regex tmp(rule, std::regex_constants::ECMAScript | std::regex_constants::icase);\n    return std::regex_search(str, tmp);\n#else\n    return str.find(rule) != std::string::npos;\n#endif\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619964927}}
{"type":"message","id":"68070032","parentId":"8b2b38e1","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:04.938Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-2b13dcddc9524fcb8792721927ae92a3","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#pragma once\n\n#include <cgnslib.h>\n\n#include <string>\n#include <limits>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <unordered_map>\n#include <map>\n#include <set>\n\nusing std::set;\nusing std::map;\nusing std::string;\nusing std::vector;\nusing std::unordered_map;\n\nnamespace MeshCut\n{\nvoid check(const int runCode, string&& errStr=\"\", string&& okStr=\"\");\n\nvoid check(bool state, string&& errStr=\"\", string&& okStr=\"\");\n\nclass CGFile\n{\npublic:\n    static const unordered_map<ElementType_t, vector<vector<int>>> nodeIdToBuildFaceInCell;\n    struct Section;\n\nprivate:\n    struct Face;\n    struct CellLoader;\n\npublic:\n    CGFile(string filename, int mode = CG_MODE_READ);\n\npublic:\n    static vector<vector<cgsize_t>> allFaceInCell(const vector<cgsize_t>& idList, ElementType_t type);\n\n    Section& addSection();\n\n    Section bodySection();\n\n    const vector<int>& bodySectionIdList() const;\n\n    const vector<int>& bdySectionIdList() const;\n\n    static ElementType_t CellType(const int nodeCnt, const int dim);\n\n    static ElementType_t CellType(const int CGNSCellTypeFalg);\n\n    void close();\n\n    vector<vector<double>> loadCoordinate(const cgsize_t rangeMin, const cgsize_t rangeMax);\n    \n    Section& loadSection(const int id);\n\n    Section& loadSection(const int id, const cgsize_t start, const cgsize_t end);\n        \n    Section loadSection(const vector<int> idList);\n\n    Section loadSection(const vector<int> idList, const cgsize_t start, const cgsize_t end);\n\n    cgsize_t nCell() const;\n\n    void nCell(const cgsize_t ncell);\n\n    cgsize_t nNode() const;\n\n    void nNode(const cgsize_t nnode);\n\n    Section& section(const int id);\n\n    static string stringAFace(const vector<cgsize_t>& face);\n\n    void writeCoordinate(const vector<vector<double>>& data);\n\n    void writeSection(Section &curS, const map<cgsize_t, cgsize_t>& nodeIdG2L);\n\n    void writeGlobalInfo(const Section &curS, const cgsize_t n, const cgsize_t start, const cgsize_t end);\n\npublic:\n    struct Section\n    {\n        int id = 1;\n        char name[33] = {0};\n        ElementType_t cellType;\n        cgsize_t start=1, end=1, dataSize=0;\n        int nBdy=0;\n        vector<vector<cgsize_t>> data;\n        vector<cgsize_t> offset{0}, typeFlag;\n        map<cgsize_t, cgsize_t> IDMap;\n\n        void addCell(const cgsize_t id, const vector<cgsize_t>& idList, const cgsize_t flag = CG_Null);\n        void clear();\n        const vector<cgsize_t>& cell(const int id) const;\n        cgsize_t flag(const int id) const;\n        bool isMixed() const;\n        void printToFile(string fname);\n        Section subSection(const cgsize_t beg, const cgsize_t end);\n    };\n\nprivate:\n    struct Face\n    {\n        Face(const vector<cgsize_t>& val);\n        Face(vector<cgsize_t>&& val) noexcept;\n        \n        Face(const Face& other);\n        Face(Face&& other) noexcept;\n\n        Face& operator=(const Face& other);\n        Face& operator=(Face&& other) noexcept;\n\n        vector<cgsize_t> nodeList; \n        bool operator==(const Face &other) const;\n        bool operator<(const Face& rhs) const;\n    };\n\n    struct CellLoader\n    {\n        /**\n         * @brief Construct a new Cell Loader object\n         * \n         * @param curS CGNS 文件结构 Section 引用\n         * @param fp 文件句柄\n         * @param lowerBound 当前 Section 的 ID 下界，包含该边界值 \n         * @param upperBound 当前 Section 的 ID 上界，包含该边界值\n         */\n        CellLoader(Section& curS, const int fp, const cgsize_t lowerBound = -1, const cgsize_t upperBound = std::numeric_limits<cgsize_t>::max());\n\n        /**\n         * @brief 将入参赋值为当前加载器构造时指定范围内的组成下一个Cell的 Node ID\n         * \n         * @param nodes Node 列表引用\n         * @return true 赋值成功\n         * @return false 已达到末尾，nodes 不可用\n         */\n        bool nextCell(vector<cgsize_t>& nodes);\n        \n        int flag = CG_Null;\n\n    private:\n        Section& curS;\n        vector<cgsize_t> data;\n        vector<cgsize_t> offset;\n        int fp, npe;\n        cgsize_t id = 0, blockSize = 0, start = 0, blockId = 0, len = 0, lowerBound = 0, upperBound = 0;\n        bool loadNextBlock();\n    };\n\nprivate:\n    string filename_;\n    int fp;\n    map<int, int> smallFiles_;\n    bool isOpen_ = false;\n\n    int cell_dim_, ibase=1, izone=1, igrid=1, ncoords;\n    cgsize_t zoneInfo[3] = {0, 0, 0};\n    DataType_t coordDataType_;\n    vector<string> coordname_;\n    \n    map<int, cgsize_t> globalNumber_;\n    map<int, cgsize_t> globalOffset_;\n    map<int, Section> sections_;\n    vector<int> bodySectionIDList_, bdySectionIDList_, interiorSection_;\n    cgsize_t idOffset_ = 1;\n\nprivate:\n\n    void checkFile();\n\n    bool isBodySection(const Section& section);\n\n    void loadCoordinateInfo();\n\n    void loadCGIOInfo();\n\n    int precision();\n};\n} // namespace MeshCut\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619964935}}
{"type":"message","id":"abcca336","parentId":"68070032","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:28.206Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"现在我有了代码的完整理解。让我分析三个核心问题：\n\n## 问题分析\n\n### P0 - distribute() 负载不均衡\n\n```cpp\nstatic std::vector<idx_t> distribute(const idx_t len, const int n, idx_t &nCellThisRank)\n{\n    vector<idx_t> dist(n + 1, 0);\n    idx_t base = len / n;\n    idx_t remainder = len % n;\n    int rank;\n    MPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &rank);\n    nCellThisRank = base + (rank < remainder ? 1 : 0);\n    MPI_Allgather(&nCellThisRank, 1, GetMPIDataType<idx_t>(), dist.data() + 1, 1, GetMPIDataType<idx_t>(), MPI_COMM_WORLD);\n    for (int iProc = 1; iProc < n + 1; ++iProc) { dist[iProc] += dist[iProc - 1]; }\n    return dist;\n}\n```\n\n这个函数的问题：\n1. 简单的静态分布策略：base + (rank < remainder ? 1 : 0)\n2. 没有考虑 ParMETIS 剖分后的实际负载\n3. 数据分布后没有动态调整机制\n\nParMETIS 已经做了图划分，但 distribute() 函数是用于划分后数据分发，问题在于：\n- 剖分后每个 part 的 cell 数量可能不均匀\n- distribute() 使用的是简单的索引范围划分，不考虑实际数据分布\n\n\n- collect_subBody 中的通信模式也可能引入额外的不平衡\n\n### P0 - Interface 全量广播 O(SIZE²)\n\n原来的 collect_interface() 使用 `MPI_Allgatherv`，现在的版本虽然改为非阻塞点对点通信，但仍有问题：\n1. 每个进程都要和所有其他进程通信 → O(SIZE²) 通信量\n2. 通信模式是全连接，即使很多进程没有 Interface 数据也要参与\n3. 序列化方式效率低（字符串拼接）\n\nInterface 的处理逻辑是用字符串格式传输，接收时需要解析字符串，这增加了序列化开销。\n\n### P1 - Interface 字符串匹配效率低\n\n在 collect_interface() 中，packInterface/unpackInterface 使用字符串拼接/解析的方式，每个进程的 Interface 数据都需要字符串化后发送，接收端需要字符串分割和解析。\n\nhashFace 函数虽然在查找时用哈希，但数据传输仍然是字符串格式。 outerFace_ 的数据结构采用嵌套的 unordered_map，查找效率应该是 O(1)，不过序列化开销较大。现在需要搜索 ParMETIS 官方文档、MPI 通信优化最佳实践，以及网格剖分并行化的相关信息。","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"现在我已理解代码结构，开始进行技术调研："},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-e010a9beeee54a2fb778c3e6bc52aa95","name":"web_search","arguments":{"query":"ParMETIS load balancing graph partitioning parallel mesh documentation","count":10}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-dd606821fb874aa48ef3aacabbb18ba0","name":"web_search","arguments":{"query":"MPI communication optimization sparse data all-to-all pattern best practices","count":10}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-c0403504f75d45748382300b74889bf8","name":"web_search","arguments":{"query":"parallel mesh partitioning interface communication optimization CFD","count":10}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":11690,"output":1231,"cacheRead":13794,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":26715,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1772619964938}}
{"type":"message","id":"4e7c2770","parentId":"abcca336","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:29.750Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-e010a9beeee54a2fb778c3e6bc52aa95","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"ParMETIS load balancing graph partitioning parallel mesh documentation\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1536,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8e6eb52abb85e697\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n(PDF) Parmetis: Parallel graph partitioning and sparse matrix ordering library\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8e6eb52abb85e697\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238705993_Parmetis_Parallel_graph_partitioning_and_sparse_matrix_ordering_library\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6ab50e0db39214c3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>ParMETIS V3 AdaptiveRepart can be used to load balance the mesh either before or after mesh adaptation</strong>. In · the latter case, each processor ﬁrst locally adapts its mesh, leading to different processors having different numbers of · elements.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6ab50e0db39214c3\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 1, 1997\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"687e5804a997d037\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nZoltan User's Guide: ParMETIS Interface\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"687e5804a997d037\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://sandialabs.github.io/Zoltan/ug_html/ug_alg_parmetis.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ba4f98a3c9080763\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThese methods are well suited for dynamic load balancing (for example, adaptive mesh refinement). A reasonable strategy is to call PartKway once to obtain a good initial balance and later update this balance using AdaptiveRepart. Zoltan supports the multiconstraint partitioning feature of ParMETIS through multiple object weights (see OBJ_WEIGHT_DIM). The graph given to Zoltan/ParMETIS must be symmetric.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ba4f98a3c9080763\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"sandialabs.github.io\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d5bf8a20ec0b90e6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n32. — Mesh Partitioning and Load Balancing — 32. 32-1 Sabina Rips\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d5bf8a20ec0b90e6\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://fs.hlrs.de/projects/par/par_prog_ws/pdf/lastverteilung_rips_engl.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d01faf0a0232da08\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n• initial graph partitioning · • dynamic load distribution · • serial and parallel · • http://www.gre.ac.uk/jostle · • HLRS: http://www.hlrs.de/organization/par/ services/tools/loadbalancer/jostle.html · • Metis: (George Karypis, University of Minnesota) • multilevel partitioning algorithms · • initial partitioning · • serial · • (parMetis: parallel version) • http://www.cs.umn.edu/~karypis/metis/metis.html ·\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d01faf0a0232da08\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"fs.hlrs.de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b53f11f38ec192f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGitHub - KarypisLab/ParMETIS: ParMETIS - Parallel Graph Partitioning and Fill-reducing Matrix Ordering\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b53f11f38ec192f\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/KarypisLab/ParMETIS\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"996be1b372aaab02\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe algorithms implemented in ParMETIS are based on the multilevel recursive-bisection, multilevel k-way, and multi-constraint partitioning schemes developed in our lab. You can download ParMETIS by simply cloning it using the command: git clone https://github.com/KarypisLab/ParMETIS.git · To build ParMETIS you can follow the instructions below: General dependencies for building ParMETIS are: gcc, cmake, build-essential, and an MPI library.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"996be1b372aaab02\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"github.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9aa1d04c45948f8f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nParMETIS - Mesh Graph Partitioning Algorithm - Technology Commercialization\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9aa1d04c45948f8f\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://license.umn.edu/product/parmetis---mesh-graph-partitioning-algorithm\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"230e2b7b3234190c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn particular, ParMETIS provides the following five major functions: ... Takes advantage of geometry information (when available) to reduce the partitioning time without loss in quality · Can partition graphs for multi-phase and multi-physics computations ... Computes high quality partitionings of very large meshes directly, without requiring the application to create the underlying graph · Provides highly efficient parallel routines for generating the dual graph of a mesh\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"230e2b7b3234190c\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"license.umn.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"204f4d8e532dd301\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nParallel Mesh Partitioning in Alya\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"204f4d8e532dd301\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://prace-ri.eu/wp-content/uploads/WP202.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b860b38267f8f32a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHowever, with parmetis and 20 partitioning workers the distribution is from 52500 to 65000 elements, and with 40 partitioning workers we obtained a distribution from 47500 to 65000. That means that if more partitioning workers are used to partition the mesh we obtain less balanced ... The conclusion is that we have to assign as few partitioning workers as possible. The minimum ... We have obtained a very good Alya scalability with the parallel ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b860b38267f8f32a\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"prace-ri.eu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d25ffb752b4224c0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEfficient Parallel Graph Partitioning in C Using ParMETIS on Clusters | IT trip\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d25ffb752b4224c0\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.ittrip.xyz/c-language/c-parmetis-parallel-partitioning\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1b978313c0e28ac0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n&lt;h3&gt;Case Study: Adaptive Mesh ... requiring frequent repartitioning. <strong>Using dynamic graph repartitioning in ParMETIS ensures efficient load balancing with minimal overhead</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1b978313c0e28ac0\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 30, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.ittrip.xyz\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fd1698765decc9dd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nParmetis parallel graph partitioning and sparse matrix ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fd1698765decc9dd\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Parmetis-parallel-graph-partitioning-and-sparse-Karypis-Schloegel/46fe89ee18398d47d89c7abb91755ad52345cf0d\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"343197b7d42e861d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>This work presents a new scratch-remap scheme called Locally-matched Multilevel Scratch- Remap (or simply LMSR) for repartitioning of adaptive meshes</strong>, and shows that it generally decreases the data redistribution costs required to balance the ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"343197b7d42e861d\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.semanticscholar.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1743d3cceddbbf59\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA general-purpose hierarchical mesh partitioning\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1743d3cceddbbf59\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.02666\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6f605ea2242ec03f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nto the dual graph of a ﬁnite element mesh) into np subgraphs · of nearly equal size, while minimizing the number of edge-cuts · required to do so. In this context, np is the number of processor · cores, the size of a processor’s subgraph is proportional to its · computational workload (thus, producing equal size subgraphs · is sometimes referred to as “load balancing”), and the num-\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6f605ea2242ec03f\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9ca4d302831d6860\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nParMETIS — Sheffield HPC Documentation\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9ca4d302831d6860\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://docs.hpc.shef.ac.uk/en/latest/stanage/software/stacks/el7-icelake-znver-stanage/Math/ParMETIS.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2c06413c72303e94\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>The algorithms implemented in ParMETIS are based on the parallel multilevel k-way graph-partitioning, adaptive repartitioning, and parallel multi-constrained partitioning schemes</strong>. ... This is an autogenerated page, more detail including examples may be available for this package.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2c06413c72303e94\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"docs.hpc.shef.ac.uk\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"details":{"query":"ParMETIS load balancing graph partitioning parallel mesh documentation","provider":"brave","count":10,"tookMs":1536,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"web_search","provider":"brave","wrapped":true},"results":[{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8e6eb52abb85e697\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n(PDF) Parmetis: Parallel graph partitioning and sparse matrix ordering library\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8e6eb52abb85e697\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238705993_Parmetis_Parallel_graph_partitioning_and_sparse_matrix_ordering_library","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6ab50e0db39214c3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>ParMETIS V3 AdaptiveRepart can be used to load balance the mesh either before or after mesh adaptation</strong>. In · the latter case, each processor ﬁrst locally adapts its mesh, leading to different processors having different numbers of · elements.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6ab50e0db39214c3\">>>","published":"January 1, 1997","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"687e5804a997d037\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nZoltan User's Guide: ParMETIS Interface\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"687e5804a997d037\">>>","url":"https://sandialabs.github.io/Zoltan/ug_html/ug_alg_parmetis.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ba4f98a3c9080763\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThese methods are well suited for dynamic load balancing (for example, adaptive mesh refinement). A reasonable strategy is to call PartKway once to obtain a good initial balance and later update this balance using AdaptiveRepart. Zoltan supports the multiconstraint partitioning feature of ParMETIS through multiple object weights (see OBJ_WEIGHT_DIM). The graph given to Zoltan/ParMETIS must be symmetric.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ba4f98a3c9080763\">>>","siteName":"sandialabs.github.io"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d5bf8a20ec0b90e6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n32. — Mesh Partitioning and Load Balancing — 32. 32-1 Sabina Rips\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d5bf8a20ec0b90e6\">>>","url":"https://fs.hlrs.de/projects/par/par_prog_ws/pdf/lastverteilung_rips_engl.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d01faf0a0232da08\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n• initial graph partitioning · • dynamic load distribution · • serial and parallel · • http://www.gre.ac.uk/jostle · • HLRS: http://www.hlrs.de/organization/par/ services/tools/loadbalancer/jostle.html · • Metis: (George Karypis, University of Minnesota) • multilevel partitioning algorithms · • initial partitioning · • serial · • (parMetis: parallel version) • http://www.cs.umn.edu/~karypis/metis/metis.html ·\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d01faf0a0232da08\">>>","siteName":"fs.hlrs.de"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b53f11f38ec192f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGitHub - KarypisLab/ParMETIS: ParMETIS - Parallel Graph Partitioning and Fill-reducing Matrix Ordering\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b53f11f38ec192f\">>>","url":"https://github.com/KarypisLab/ParMETIS","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"996be1b372aaab02\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe algorithms implemented in ParMETIS are based on the multilevel recursive-bisection, multilevel k-way, and multi-constraint partitioning schemes developed in our lab. You can download ParMETIS by simply cloning it using the command: git clone https://github.com/KarypisLab/ParMETIS.git · To build ParMETIS you can follow the instructions below: General dependencies for building ParMETIS are: gcc, cmake, build-essential, and an MPI library.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"996be1b372aaab02\">>>","siteName":"github.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9aa1d04c45948f8f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nParMETIS - Mesh Graph Partitioning Algorithm - Technology Commercialization\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9aa1d04c45948f8f\">>>","url":"https://license.umn.edu/product/parmetis---mesh-graph-partitioning-algorithm","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"230e2b7b3234190c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIn particular, ParMETIS provides the following five major functions: ... Takes advantage of geometry information (when available) to reduce the partitioning time without loss in quality · Can partition graphs for multi-phase and multi-physics computations ... Computes high quality partitionings of very large meshes directly, without requiring the application to create the underlying graph · Provides highly efficient parallel routines for generating the dual graph of a mesh\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"230e2b7b3234190c\">>>","siteName":"license.umn.edu"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"204f4d8e532dd301\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nParallel Mesh Partitioning in Alya\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"204f4d8e532dd301\">>>","url":"https://prace-ri.eu/wp-content/uploads/WP202.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b860b38267f8f32a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHowever, with parmetis and 20 partitioning workers the distribution is from 52500 to 65000 elements, and with 40 partitioning workers we obtained a distribution from 47500 to 65000. That means that if more partitioning workers are used to partition the mesh we obtain less balanced ... The conclusion is that we have to assign as few partitioning workers as possible. The minimum ... We have obtained a very good Alya scalability with the parallel ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b860b38267f8f32a\">>>","siteName":"prace-ri.eu"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d25ffb752b4224c0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEfficient Parallel Graph Partitioning in C Using ParMETIS on Clusters | IT trip\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d25ffb752b4224c0\">>>","url":"https://en.ittrip.xyz/c-language/c-parmetis-parallel-partitioning","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1b978313c0e28ac0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n&lt;h3&gt;Case Study: Adaptive Mesh ... requiring frequent repartitioning. <strong>Using dynamic graph repartitioning in ParMETIS ensures efficient load balancing with minimal overhead</strong>....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1b978313c0e28ac0\">>>","published":"December 30, 2024","siteName":"en.ittrip.xyz"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fd1698765decc9dd\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nParmetis parallel graph partitioning and sparse matrix ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fd1698765decc9dd\">>>","url":"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Parmetis-parallel-graph-partitioning-and-sparse-Karypis-Schloegel/46fe89ee18398d47d89c7abb91755ad52345cf0d","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"343197b7d42e861d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>This work presents a new scratch-remap scheme called Locally-matched Multilevel Scratch- Remap (or simply LMSR) for repartitioning of adaptive meshes</strong>, and shows that it generally decreases the data redistribution costs required to balance the ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"343197b7d42e861d\">>>","siteName":"www.semanticscholar.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1743d3cceddbbf59\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nA general-purpose hierarchical mesh partitioning\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1743d3cceddbbf59\">>>","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.02666","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6f605ea2242ec03f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nto the dual graph of a ﬁnite element mesh) into np subgraphs · of nearly equal size, while minimizing the number of edge-cuts · required to do so. In this context, np is the number of processor · cores, the size of a processor’s subgraph is proportional to its · computational workload (thus, producing equal size subgraphs · is sometimes referred to as “load balancing”), and the num-\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6f605ea2242ec03f\">>>","siteName":"arxiv.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9ca4d302831d6860\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nParMETIS — Sheffield HPC Documentation\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9ca4d302831d6860\">>>","url":"https://docs.hpc.shef.ac.uk/en/latest/stanage/software/stacks/el7-icelake-znver-stanage/Math/ParMETIS.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2c06413c72303e94\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>The algorithms implemented in ParMETIS are based on the parallel multilevel k-way graph-partitioning, adaptive repartitioning, and parallel multi-constrained partitioning schemes</strong>. ... This is an autogenerated page, more detail including examples may be available for this package.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2c06413c72303e94\">>>","siteName":"docs.hpc.shef.ac.uk"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619989747}}
{"type":"message","id":"5a107741","parentId":"4e7c2770","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:31.137Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-dd606821fb874aa48ef3aacabbb18ba0","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"MPI communication optimization sparse data all-to-all pattern best practices\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1380,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dae7c35b82e05896\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDynamic Sparse Data Exchange with MPI_IBARRIER | MPI Performance Guidelines\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dae7c35b82e05896\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://mpi-performance-guidelines.github.io/dynamic-sparse\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"46588d89b27f2ecf\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor users with sparse data, users might often look to MPI_ALLTOALLV to <strong>lighten the communication load and memory consumption during communication</strong>. MPI_ALLTOALLV allows each process to contribute different amounts of data to the collective. In a sparse exchange, many processes may contribute ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"46588d89b27f2ecf\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"mpi-performance-guidelines.github.io\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e86cd8891f07188\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSparse Collective Operations for MPI Torsten Hoeﬂer Open Systems Lab\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e86cd8891f07188\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-sparsecolls.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5c90a902b9d71b5c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nto convey information of optimization criteria, process arrival · patterns and other information that could be relevant to the · MPI implementation. The weight arguments should reﬂect · (approximately) the amount of communication with neighbors,\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5c90a902b9d71b5c\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"htor.inf.ethz.ch\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"943694abce3d3f3b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOptimization Principles for Collective Neighborhood Communications\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"943694abce3d3f3b\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-schneider-neighbor-colls.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1c9a1b31c1c2e374\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nof neighborhood or sparse collective operation itself is new, we were able to borrow several algorithmic principles to · drive our heuristics from established collective communication · primitives such as efﬁcient alltoall implementations [29] or ... Potluri et al. [35] demonstrated MPI One Sided tech- niques to optimize different communication patterns.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1c9a1b31c1c2e374\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"htor.inf.ethz.ch\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4184b1a010ef88c3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n(PDF) Sparse collective operations for MPI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4184b1a010ef88c3\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220949851_Sparse_collective_operations_for_MPI\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"086f5c5080417d1d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe common optimization for eliminating diagonal communication of the y regions by piggy backing could easily be done transparently to the user with a good implementation of MPI Neighbor alltoallw. ... Two dimensional mesh or torus (wrap-around edges not shown). The local neighborhood of each process indicated with heavy lines consists of the same 4 source and target processes. ... Running times for the two sparse all-to-all implementations on an NEC SX-8 node with 8 processes.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"086f5c5080417d1d\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"May 1, 2009\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a88d036a8c41012d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMPI: A Message-Passing Interface Standard Version 4.1\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a88d036a8c41012d\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mpi-forum.org/docs/mpi-4.1/mpi41-report.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0664e64f57773de3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCurrent Practice #2 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... Library Example #1 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... Library Example #2 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... Inter-Communication Examples . . . . . . . .\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0664e64f57773de3\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.mpi-forum.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"63082c9b4b6e6bed\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nKokkos MPI (Message Passing Interface) :: Parallel Programming\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"63082c9b4b6e6bed\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://feelpp.github.io/course-parallel-programming/parallel-programming/kokkos/advanced-concepts/mpi.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d459e710af4b0463\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nKokkos::View&lt;double*&gt; ... and can leverage the full parallelism of the underlying hardware. For sparse communication patterns, <strong>generating efficient index lists</strong> is crucial....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d459e710af4b0463\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"feelpp.github.io\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e91df11bb491e01c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDesign and Optimization of GPU-Aware MPI Allreduce Using Direct Sendrecv Communication | Proceedings of the 54th International Conference on Parallel Processing\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e91df11bb491e01c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3754598.3754666\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1c5978731df82af5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor instance, the Ring algorithm is typically more efficient for large messages in GPU reductions, while Recursive Halving and Recursive Doubling (RD) are preferred for smaller-scale Reduce-Scatter and Allgather operations. In this work, we propose the use of a Direct Sendrecv algorithm with throttling mechanisms, inspired by the Alltoall communication pattern, to redesign both the Reduce-Scatter and Allgather phases.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1c5978731df82af5\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"dl.acm.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d4b2026cd22f9ed2\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMPI Advance : Open-Source Message Passing Optimizations\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d4b2026cd22f9ed2\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.07337\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ef6d084106aa098f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nNeighborhood collectives allow MPI to optimize sparse communication, in which each process communicates with a\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ef6d084106aa098f\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"24656925a247e929\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOptimization of Collective Communication Operations in MPICH. | Request PDF\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"24656925a247e929\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220457366_Optimization_of_Collective_Communication_Operations_in_MPICH\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0171561618fe5b36\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe main contribution of this work is a new, high-performance parallel computational framework for a broad class of NMF algorithms that iteratively solves alternating non-negative least squares (NLS) subproblems for W and H. It maintains the data and factor matrices in memory (distributed across processors), uses MPI for interprocessor communication, and, in the dense case, provably minimizes communication costs (under mild assumptions). The framework is flexible and able to leverage a variety of NMF and NLS algorithms, including Multiplicative Update, Hierarchical Alternating Least Squares, and Block Principal Pivoting. Our implementation allows us to benchmark and compare different algorithms on massive dense and sparse data matrices of size that spans for few hundreds of millions to billions.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0171561618fe5b36\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 1, 2005\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"99205d2d2eb90446\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nmpi - Is there an implementation of MPI_AllReduce which handles sparse data better? - Computational Science Stack Exchange\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"99205d2d2eb90446\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://scicomp.stackexchange.com/questions/21581/is-there-an-implementation-of-mpi-allreduce-which-handles-sparse-data-better\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"62b9987bfc1a20c0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n$\\\\begingroup$ If you use derived data types, you have to roll your own reduction operators, which will disable all of the optimizations that go into most MPI implementations. $\\\\endgroup$ ... $\\\\begingroup$ Thanks Bill for your suggestion. It seems that the solution vector is 1,355,191 entries 60-70% of them are zero. The MPI communication is the main bottleneck.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"62b9987bfc1a20c0\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"scicomp.stackexchange.com\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"details":{"query":"MPI communication optimization sparse data all-to-all pattern best practices","provider":"brave","count":10,"tookMs":1380,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"web_search","provider":"brave","wrapped":true},"results":[{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dae7c35b82e05896\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDynamic Sparse Data Exchange with MPI_IBARRIER | MPI Performance Guidelines\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dae7c35b82e05896\">>>","url":"https://mpi-performance-guidelines.github.io/dynamic-sparse","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"46588d89b27f2ecf\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor users with sparse data, users might often look to MPI_ALLTOALLV to <strong>lighten the communication load and memory consumption during communication</strong>. MPI_ALLTOALLV allows each process to contribute different amounts of data to the collective. In a sparse exchange, many processes may contribute ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"46588d89b27f2ecf\">>>","siteName":"mpi-performance-guidelines.github.io"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7e86cd8891f07188\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSparse Collective Operations for MPI Torsten Hoeﬂer Open Systems Lab\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7e86cd8891f07188\">>>","url":"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-sparsecolls.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5c90a902b9d71b5c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nto convey information of optimization criteria, process arrival · patterns and other information that could be relevant to the · MPI implementation. The weight arguments should reﬂect · (approximately) the amount of communication with neighbors,\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5c90a902b9d71b5c\">>>","siteName":"htor.inf.ethz.ch"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"943694abce3d3f3b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOptimization Principles for Collective Neighborhood Communications\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"943694abce3d3f3b\">>>","url":"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-schneider-neighbor-colls.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1c9a1b31c1c2e374\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nof neighborhood or sparse collective operation itself is new, we were able to borrow several algorithmic principles to · drive our heuristics from established collective communication · primitives such as efﬁcient alltoall implementations [29] or ... Potluri et al. [35] demonstrated MPI One Sided tech- niques to optimize different communication patterns.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1c9a1b31c1c2e374\">>>","siteName":"htor.inf.ethz.ch"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4184b1a010ef88c3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n(PDF) Sparse collective operations for MPI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4184b1a010ef88c3\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220949851_Sparse_collective_operations_for_MPI","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"086f5c5080417d1d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe common optimization for eliminating diagonal communication of the y regions by piggy backing could easily be done transparently to the user with a good implementation of MPI Neighbor alltoallw. ... Two dimensional mesh or torus (wrap-around edges not shown). The local neighborhood of each process indicated with heavy lines consists of the same 4 source and target processes. ... Running times for the two sparse all-to-all implementations on an NEC SX-8 node with 8 processes.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"086f5c5080417d1d\">>>","published":"May 1, 2009","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a88d036a8c41012d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMPI: A Message-Passing Interface Standard Version 4.1\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a88d036a8c41012d\">>>","url":"https://www.mpi-forum.org/docs/mpi-4.1/mpi41-report.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0664e64f57773de3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nCurrent Practice #2 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... Library Example #1 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... Library Example #2 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ... Inter-Communication Examples . . . . . . . .\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0664e64f57773de3\">>>","siteName":"www.mpi-forum.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"63082c9b4b6e6bed\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nKokkos MPI (Message Passing Interface) :: Parallel Programming\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"63082c9b4b6e6bed\">>>","url":"https://feelpp.github.io/course-parallel-programming/parallel-programming/kokkos/advanced-concepts/mpi.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d459e710af4b0463\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nKokkos::View&lt;double*&gt; ... and can leverage the full parallelism of the underlying hardware. For sparse communication patterns, <strong>generating efficient index lists</strong> is crucial....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d459e710af4b0463\">>>","siteName":"feelpp.github.io"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e91df11bb491e01c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDesign and Optimization of GPU-Aware MPI Allreduce Using Direct Sendrecv Communication | Proceedings of the 54th International Conference on Parallel Processing\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e91df11bb491e01c\">>>","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3754598.3754666","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1c5978731df82af5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor instance, the Ring algorithm is typically more efficient for large messages in GPU reductions, while Recursive Halving and Recursive Doubling (RD) are preferred for smaller-scale Reduce-Scatter and Allgather operations. In this work, we propose the use of a Direct Sendrecv algorithm with throttling mechanisms, inspired by the Alltoall communication pattern, to redesign both the Reduce-Scatter and Allgather phases.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1c5978731df82af5\">>>","siteName":"dl.acm.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d4b2026cd22f9ed2\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMPI Advance : Open-Source Message Passing Optimizations\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d4b2026cd22f9ed2\">>>","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.07337","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ef6d084106aa098f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nNeighborhood collectives allow MPI to optimize sparse communication, in which each process communicates with a\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ef6d084106aa098f\">>>","siteName":"arxiv.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"24656925a247e929\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOptimization of Collective Communication Operations in MPICH. | Request PDF\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"24656925a247e929\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220457366_Optimization_of_Collective_Communication_Operations_in_MPICH","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0171561618fe5b36\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe main contribution of this work is a new, high-performance parallel computational framework for a broad class of NMF algorithms that iteratively solves alternating non-negative least squares (NLS) subproblems for W and H. It maintains the data and factor matrices in memory (distributed across processors), uses MPI for interprocessor communication, and, in the dense case, provably minimizes communication costs (under mild assumptions). The framework is flexible and able to leverage a variety of NMF and NLS algorithms, including Multiplicative Update, Hierarchical Alternating Least Squares, and Block Principal Pivoting. Our implementation allows us to benchmark and compare different algorithms on massive dense and sparse data matrices of size that spans for few hundreds of millions to billions.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0171561618fe5b36\">>>","published":"January 1, 2005","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"99205d2d2eb90446\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nmpi - Is there an implementation of MPI_AllReduce which handles sparse data better? - Computational Science Stack Exchange\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"99205d2d2eb90446\">>>","url":"https://scicomp.stackexchange.com/questions/21581/is-there-an-implementation-of-mpi-allreduce-which-handles-sparse-data-better","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"62b9987bfc1a20c0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n$\\begingroup$ If you use derived data types, you have to roll your own reduction operators, which will disable all of the optimizations that go into most MPI implementations. $\\endgroup$ ... $\\begingroup$ Thanks Bill for your suggestion. It seems that the solution vector is 1,355,191 entries 60-70% of them are zero. The MPI communication is the main bottleneck.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"62b9987bfc1a20c0\">>>","siteName":"scicomp.stackexchange.com"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619991135}}
{"type":"message","id":"9a663a92","parentId":"5a107741","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:32.431Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-c0403504f75d45748382300b74889bf8","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"parallel mesh partitioning interface communication optimization CFD\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1282,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"602706c5147cd761\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nImpact of mesh partitioning methods in CFD for large scale parallel computing - ScienceDirect\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"602706c5147cd761\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0045793014003016\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6cb56e18b58aadb1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA well balanced load can improve efficiency of parallel computing and minimizing the number of cells with remote neighbors can reduce the time spent on the data communication among processors. Mesh partitioning methods were able to optimize the mesh cell distributions for large scale parallel computing. In this paper, the open source mesh partitioning software packages, such as Metis, ParMetis, Scotch, PT-Scotch and Zoltan, were ported into Code_Saturne, which is an open source CFD software package, for realizing the optimization on large scale CFD applications.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6cb56e18b58aadb1\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"July 24, 2014\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.sciencedirect.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd16614dfdb77ad1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLarge‐Scale CFD Parallel Computing Dealing with Massive Mesh - Shang - 2013 - Journal of Engineering - Wiley Online Library\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd16614dfdb77ad1\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2013/850148\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"15036d17537da7ff\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThey are simple mesh partitioning [1] and space-filling curve (SFC) method [2]. The simple mesh partitioning actually did not perform any optimization on the mesh distributions. Figure 4 shows the 3D mesh partitioning results on 4 processors for the DARPA submarine. It can be seen that Metis, ParMetis, Scotch, PT-Scotch, and Zoltan (RIB) can produce the neat inner boundaries among the processors. Therefore, they can have fewer neighbor processors. It will be in merit for the data communication during the parallel computing.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"15036d17537da7ff\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 28, 2013\",\n      \"siteName\": \"onlinelibrary.wiley.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a7cad164b7897da1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMesh partitioning using matrix value approximations for parallel computational fluid dynamics simulations - Miao Wang, Xinhai Xu, Xiaoguang Ren, Chao Li, Juan Chen, Xuejun Yang, 2017\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a7cad164b7897da1\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/1687814017734109\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b5778a38b05dd6f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor CFD problems using finite volume method (FVM), we analyze the discretization of a PDE into a linear system and find out that the areas of the interfaces are appropriate approximations for the matrix off-diagonal coefficients. A generalized partitioning model combining load balance, communication volume, and convergence feature using the matrix value approximations is built.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b5778a38b05dd6f\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"journals.sagepub.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"796f0ae879de4327\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMesh Partitioning - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"796f0ae879de4327\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/mesh-partitioning\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f27abb879560ef24\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe interfaces between partitioned blocks are of matching and overlapping type [8]. They exchange data between the blocks. During a parallel run of the dynamic mesh algorithm, <strong>the code executes on each of the partitioned blocks to solve the relevant equations for that particular subdomain</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f27abb879560ef24\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.sciencedirect.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e2495960e5e0daf7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nImpact of mesh partitioning methods in CFD for large scale parallel computing | Request PDF\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e2495960e5e0daf7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/264418667_Impact_of_mesh_partitioning_methods_in_CFD_for_large_scale_parallel_computing\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a74312475ef90a7c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>The communication costs of mapping this partitioning onto different microprocessors: a mesh-connected array, a tree machine and a hypercube is then studied</strong>. The communication cost expressions can be used to determine the optimal depth of the ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a74312475ef90a7c\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 1, 2014\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9f279d79358d2cfa\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn order to run CFD codes more efficiently on large scales, the parallel computing has to be employed. For example, in industrial scales, it usually uses tens of thousands of mesh cells to capture the details of complex geometries. How to distribute these mesh cells among the multiprocessors for obtaining a good parallel computing performance (HPC) is really a challenge. Due to dealing with the massive mesh cells, it is difficult for the CFD codes without parallel optimizations to handle this kind of large-\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9f279d79358d2cfa\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/je/2013/850148/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5b3757769f849d89\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThey are simple mesh partitioning [1] and space-filling curve (SFC) method [2]. The simple mesh partitioning actually did not perform any optimization on the mesh distributions. Figure 4 shows the 3D mesh partitioning results on 4 processors for the DARPA submarine. It can be seen that Metis, ParMetis, Scotch, PT-Scotch, and Zoltan (RIB) can produce the neat inner boundaries among the processors. Therefore, they can have fewer neighbor processors. It will be in merit for the data communication during the parallel computing.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5b3757769f849d89\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 28, 2013\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.hindawi.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6288f1ac896045c6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMesh-Partitioning Metrics for Parallel Preconditioned Conjugated Gradient Solvers in CFD | Request PDF\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6288f1ac896045c6\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/309688381_Mesh-Partitioning_Metrics_for_Parallel_Preconditioned_Conjugated_Gradient_Solvers_in_CFD\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"24bf4169013a0438\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>Generated mesh partitions can be smoothed and optimized for minimum interface and maximum load balance using one of several non-deterministic optimization algorithms</strong>. TOP/DOMDEC also provides real-time means for assessing a priori the quality ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"24bf4169013a0438\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"July 1, 2016\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dc4d2796c2d17422\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMesh-Partitioning Metrics for Parallel Preconditioned Conjugated Gradient Solvers in CFD | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dc4d2796c2d17422\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/7726201/;jsessionid=3A6AF8F31AC770F49EEC71E64387B3EE\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3ed53c3a504cdbce\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>Mesh partitioning has a significant influence on the efficiency of parallel preconditioned conjugated gradient (PCG) solving procedure</strong>, which is the most representative and time-consuming part in parallel CFD.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3ed53c3a504cdbce\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"ieeexplore.ieee.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"634d20bb24c252d9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHigh-Performance Computing: Dos and Don’ts | IntechOpen\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"634d20bb24c252d9\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/58618\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ef2ac86e194929ae\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nTwo properties are desired for a mesh partition, <strong>good balance of the resulting workload distribution and minimal communication requirements</strong>. However, in a CFD code, different types of operations coexist, acting on fields of different mesh dimensions ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ef2ac86e194929ae\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 20, 2017\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.intechopen.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c7d1cf5233ed7b54\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAchieving High Speed CFD simulations: Optimization,\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c7d1cf5233ed7b54\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://elib.dlr.de/57896/1/AIAA2009_AndresWidhalm.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"95de38cade21fc4a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHowever, the communication cost is not represented in the algorithm, and it is not · possible to establish several point weights to deal with diﬀerent aspects. For testing purposes, we used the DLR F6 conﬁguration. The mesh decomposition into diﬀerent domains · achieved with the geometric partitioning algorithm is shown in Figure 2 and 3. Using this domain decomposition, the parallel scalability and speedup of the TAU ﬂow solver for the F6\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"95de38cade21fc4a\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"elib.dlr.de\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"details":{"query":"parallel mesh partitioning interface communication optimization CFD","provider":"brave","count":10,"tookMs":1282,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"web_search","provider":"brave","wrapped":true},"results":[{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"602706c5147cd761\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nImpact of mesh partitioning methods in CFD for large scale parallel computing - ScienceDirect\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"602706c5147cd761\">>>","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0045793014003016","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6cb56e18b58aadb1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nA well balanced load can improve efficiency of parallel computing and minimizing the number of cells with remote neighbors can reduce the time spent on the data communication among processors. Mesh partitioning methods were able to optimize the mesh cell distributions for large scale parallel computing. In this paper, the open source mesh partitioning software packages, such as Metis, ParMetis, Scotch, PT-Scotch and Zoltan, were ported into Code_Saturne, which is an open source CFD software package, for realizing the optimization on large scale CFD applications.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6cb56e18b58aadb1\">>>","published":"July 24, 2014","siteName":"www.sciencedirect.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bd16614dfdb77ad1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLarge‐Scale CFD Parallel Computing Dealing with Massive Mesh - Shang - 2013 - Journal of Engineering - Wiley Online Library\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bd16614dfdb77ad1\">>>","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2013/850148","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"15036d17537da7ff\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThey are simple mesh partitioning [1] and space-filling curve (SFC) method [2]. The simple mesh partitioning actually did not perform any optimization on the mesh distributions. Figure 4 shows the 3D mesh partitioning results on 4 processors for the DARPA submarine. It can be seen that Metis, ParMetis, Scotch, PT-Scotch, and Zoltan (RIB) can produce the neat inner boundaries among the processors. Therefore, they can have fewer neighbor processors. It will be in merit for the data communication during the parallel computing.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"15036d17537da7ff\">>>","published":"April 28, 2013","siteName":"onlinelibrary.wiley.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a7cad164b7897da1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMesh partitioning using matrix value approximations for parallel computational fluid dynamics simulations - Miao Wang, Xinhai Xu, Xiaoguang Ren, Chao Li, Juan Chen, Xuejun Yang, 2017\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a7cad164b7897da1\">>>","url":"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/1687814017734109","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b5778a38b05dd6f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor CFD problems using finite volume method (FVM), we analyze the discretization of a PDE into a linear system and find out that the areas of the interfaces are appropriate approximations for the matrix off-diagonal coefficients. A generalized partitioning model combining load balance, communication volume, and convergence feature using the matrix value approximations is built.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b5778a38b05dd6f\">>>","siteName":"journals.sagepub.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"796f0ae879de4327\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMesh Partitioning - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"796f0ae879de4327\">>>","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/mesh-partitioning","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f27abb879560ef24\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe interfaces between partitioned blocks are of matching and overlapping type [8]. They exchange data between the blocks. During a parallel run of the dynamic mesh algorithm, <strong>the code executes on each of the partitioned blocks to solve the relevant equations for that particular subdomain</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f27abb879560ef24\">>>","siteName":"www.sciencedirect.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e2495960e5e0daf7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nImpact of mesh partitioning methods in CFD for large scale parallel computing | Request PDF\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e2495960e5e0daf7\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/264418667_Impact_of_mesh_partitioning_methods_in_CFD_for_large_scale_parallel_computing","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a74312475ef90a7c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>The communication costs of mapping this partitioning onto different microprocessors: a mesh-connected array, a tree machine and a hypercube is then studied</strong>. The communication cost expressions can be used to determine the optimal depth of the ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a74312475ef90a7c\">>>","published":"December 1, 2014","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9f279d79358d2cfa\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIn order to run CFD codes more efficiently on large scales, the parallel computing has to be employed. For example, in industrial scales, it usually uses tens of thousands of mesh cells to capture the details of complex geometries. How to distribute these mesh cells among the multiprocessors for obtaining a good parallel computing performance (HPC) is really a challenge. Due to dealing with the massive mesh cells, it is difficult for the CFD codes without parallel optimizations to handle this kind of large-\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9f279d79358d2cfa\">>>","url":"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/je/2013/850148/","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5b3757769f849d89\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThey are simple mesh partitioning [1] and space-filling curve (SFC) method [2]. The simple mesh partitioning actually did not perform any optimization on the mesh distributions. Figure 4 shows the 3D mesh partitioning results on 4 processors for the DARPA submarine. It can be seen that Metis, ParMetis, Scotch, PT-Scotch, and Zoltan (RIB) can produce the neat inner boundaries among the processors. Therefore, they can have fewer neighbor processors. It will be in merit for the data communication during the parallel computing.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5b3757769f849d89\">>>","published":"April 28, 2013","siteName":"www.hindawi.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6288f1ac896045c6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMesh-Partitioning Metrics for Parallel Preconditioned Conjugated Gradient Solvers in CFD | Request PDF\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6288f1ac896045c6\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/309688381_Mesh-Partitioning_Metrics_for_Parallel_Preconditioned_Conjugated_Gradient_Solvers_in_CFD","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"24bf4169013a0438\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>Generated mesh partitions can be smoothed and optimized for minimum interface and maximum load balance using one of several non-deterministic optimization algorithms</strong>. TOP/DOMDEC also provides real-time means for assessing a priori the quality ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"24bf4169013a0438\">>>","published":"July 1, 2016","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dc4d2796c2d17422\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMesh-Partitioning Metrics for Parallel Preconditioned Conjugated Gradient Solvers in CFD | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dc4d2796c2d17422\">>>","url":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/7726201/;jsessionid=3A6AF8F31AC770F49EEC71E64387B3EE","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3ed53c3a504cdbce\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>Mesh partitioning has a significant influence on the efficiency of parallel preconditioned conjugated gradient (PCG) solving procedure</strong>, which is the most representative and time-consuming part in parallel CFD.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3ed53c3a504cdbce\">>>","siteName":"ieeexplore.ieee.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"634d20bb24c252d9\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHigh-Performance Computing: Dos and Don’ts | IntechOpen\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"634d20bb24c252d9\">>>","url":"https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/58618","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ef2ac86e194929ae\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nTwo properties are desired for a mesh partition, <strong>good balance of the resulting workload distribution and minimal communication requirements</strong>. However, in a CFD code, different types of operations coexist, acting on fields of different mesh dimensions ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ef2ac86e194929ae\">>>","published":"December 20, 2017","siteName":"www.intechopen.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c7d1cf5233ed7b54\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nAchieving High Speed CFD simulations: Optimization,\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c7d1cf5233ed7b54\">>>","url":"https://elib.dlr.de/57896/1/AIAA2009_AndresWidhalm.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"95de38cade21fc4a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHowever, the communication cost is not represented in the algorithm, and it is not · possible to establish several point weights to deal with diﬀerent aspects. For testing purposes, we used the DLR F6 conﬁguration. The mesh decomposition into diﬀerent domains · achieved with the geometric partitioning algorithm is shown in Figure 2 and 3. Using this domain decomposition, the parallel scalability and speedup of the TAU ﬂow solver for the F6\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"95de38cade21fc4a\">>>","siteName":"elib.dlr.de"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1772619992423}}
