{"type":"message","id":"5af750a1","parentId":"811d4da0","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:44.137Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-21a01ed878ea4018994b38c4e14cfac4","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"hash-based face matching mesh data structure parallel CFD\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 2226,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f19381fa7fd9961c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCompact Array-Based Mesh Data Structures Tyler J. Alumbaugh and Xiangmin Jiao\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f19381fa7fd9961c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://cgvr.cs.uni-bremen.de/teaching/cg_literatur/Array-Based%20Mesh%20Data%20Structures.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"acbb6495a5e66296\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nKey words: mesh data structures, half-edge, half-face, parallel computing ... CFD General Notation System (CGNS), an AIAA Recommended Practice [The02].\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"acbb6495a5e66296\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"cgvr.cs.uni-bremen.de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"451661c719158eef\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAn ultralight geometry processing library for parallel mesh refinement | Advances in Aerodynamics | Full Text\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"451661c719158eef\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://aia.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s42774-021-00093-8\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d4ef8ae389cbd9f0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>Its brute-force implementations need to traverse the facet list. Therefore, a hash table is created to improve the performance of this routine, where the smallest index of the corner nodes is the key value of a facet, and the facets having an identical key value form a backup list</strong>...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d4ef8ae389cbd9f0\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"March 29, 2022\",\n      \"siteName\": \"aia.springeropen.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1e4ab50809968b88\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFace coloring in unstructured CFD codes | Parallel Computing\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1e4ab50809968b88\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1016/j.parco.2017.04.001\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a4d1b9e37442e3a3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nPrevention of race conditions in GPU codes.Edge-coloring algorithm that uses the minimum number of colors.Unstructured conforming and nonconforming geometric meshes.Optimization of memory accesses....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a4d1b9e37442e3a3\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"dl.acm.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a11bc09b64285029\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n[snappyHexMesh] SnappyHexMesh Parallel run - face ordering problem -- CFD Online Discussion Forums\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a11bc09b64285029\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cfd-online.com/Forums/openfoam-meshing/178909-snappyhexmesh-parallel-run-face-ordering-problem.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"84c24ccea73f9ac9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHi all, wondering if you can help me with a problem that has been plaguing my simulations for weeks now!! I have no problem setting up and running my\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"84c24ccea73f9ac9\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cfd-online.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"871aab2efe751f8a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\npde - Algorithm for face based data-structure - CFD - Computational Science Stack Exchange\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"871aab2efe751f8a\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://scicomp.stackexchange.com/questions/25431/algorithm-for-face-based-data-structure-cfd\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fc5c7d27bffe08ae\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n$\\\\begingroup$ I know a code that has done a very similar procedure which collects face data from unstructured mesh output from GMSH (gmsh.info). Here is it: dolfyn.net/dolfyn/downloads_en.html Authors wrote the code in Fortran90 and the code is quite simple to be read.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fc5c7d27bffe08ae\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"scicomp.stackexchange.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dcb355435c164586\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGenerating Multibillion Element Unstructured Meshes on Distributed Memory Parallel Machines - Soner - 2015 - Scientific Programming - Wiley Online Library\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dcb355435c164586\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2015/437480\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4806b3ed36c5b629\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFinally, we note that when new mesh entities are created in parallel, duplicate entities on the partition boundaries result which need to be matched. A somewhat similar problem occurs in the OpenFOAM reconstructParMesh utility. We quote Piscaglia et al. [18] on this problem: “ … the point, face and cell processor addressing lists, generated at decomposition time, are no longer valid once topological changes have occurred.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4806b3ed36c5b629\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"May 26, 2015\",\n      \"siteName\": \"onlinelibrary.wiley.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"098a195305e3f8bd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMassively Parallel CFD Simulation Software: CCFD Development and Optimization Based on Sunway TaihuLight - Liu - 2020 - Scientific Programming - Wiley Online Library\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"098a195305e3f8bd\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2020/8847481\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"31f28911c53e2294\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nUsing the multiblock structure grid parallel technology of domain decomposition, we design the parallel software framework, software process, parallel data structure, and communication mode of parallel CFD solver software. The designed CCFD can be used for large-scale mega-core parallel computing tasks and has good parallel expansion ability and parallel efficiency. The overdecomposition load balancing strategy for CCFD guarantees the load balancing performance of computing and communication. The use of a mesh sequencing method based on multigrid technology can accelerate the iterative convergence speed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"31f28911c53e2294\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"July 22, 2020\",\n      \"siteName\": \"onlinelibrary.wiley.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9ca3bc6aa8b86726\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCoherent mesh representation for parallel I/O of unstructured polyhedral meshes | The Journal of Supercomputing | Springer Nature Link\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9ca3bc6aa8b86726\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11227-024-06051-7\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"00e7490baf66035b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>This paper presents a new mesh data layout for parallel I/O of linear unstructured polyhedral meshes</strong>. The new mesh representation infers coherence across entities of different topological dimensions, i.e., grid cells, faces, and points.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"00e7490baf66035b\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 9, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"link.springer.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"537fe6dfa0d430a2\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nData Structures and Mesh Processing in Parallel CFD Project GIMM ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"537fe6dfa0d430a2\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/35010120.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f0446a452ab90305\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe world’s largest collection of open access research papers\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f0446a452ab90305\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"core.ac.uk\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f646b0c08c52046c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nfluid dynamics - What are some good data-types for unstructured cell-centered FVM CFD code? - Computational Science Stack Exchange\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f646b0c08c52046c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://scicomp.stackexchange.com/questions/4733/what-are-some-good-data-types-for-unstructured-cell-centered-fvm-cfd-code\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"578f6742fdc1ccf1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOne example that I encountered (in dolfyn cfd code) goes like this (I&#x27;ll show relevant segment) <strong>\\\\begin{listing} do ip=1,Ncel ... do j=1,NFaces(ip) k = CFace(ip,j) ipp = Face(k)�ll1 inn = Face(k)�ll2 if( inn &gt; 0 )then ! internal \\\\end{listing}</strong> So ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"578f6742fdc1ccf1\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"scicomp.stackexchange.com\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"details":{"query":"hash-based face matching mesh data structure parallel CFD","provider":"brave","count":10,"tookMs":2226,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"web_search","provider":"brave","wrapped":true},"results":[{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f19381fa7fd9961c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nCompact Array-Based Mesh Data Structures Tyler J. Alumbaugh and Xiangmin Jiao\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f19381fa7fd9961c\">>>","url":"https://cgvr.cs.uni-bremen.de/teaching/cg_literatur/Array-Based%20Mesh%20Data%20Structures.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"acbb6495a5e66296\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nKey words: mesh data structures, half-edge, half-face, parallel computing ... CFD General Notation System (CGNS), an AIAA Recommended Practice [The02].\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"acbb6495a5e66296\">>>","siteName":"cgvr.cs.uni-bremen.de"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"451661c719158eef\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nAn ultralight geometry processing library for parallel mesh refinement | Advances in Aerodynamics | Full Text\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"451661c719158eef\">>>","url":"https://aia.springeropen.com/articles/10.1186/s42774-021-00093-8","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d4ef8ae389cbd9f0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>Its brute-force implementations need to traverse the facet list. Therefore, a hash table is created to improve the performance of this routine, where the smallest index of the corner nodes is the key value of a facet, and the facets having an identical key value form a backup list</strong>...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d4ef8ae389cbd9f0\">>>","published":"March 29, 2022","siteName":"aia.springeropen.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1e4ab50809968b88\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFace coloring in unstructured CFD codes | Parallel Computing\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1e4ab50809968b88\">>>","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1016/j.parco.2017.04.001","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a4d1b9e37442e3a3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nPrevention of race conditions in GPU codes.Edge-coloring algorithm that uses the minimum number of colors.Unstructured conforming and nonconforming geometric meshes.Optimization of memory accesses....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a4d1b9e37442e3a3\">>>","siteName":"dl.acm.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a11bc09b64285029\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n[snappyHexMesh] SnappyHexMesh Parallel run - face ordering problem -- CFD Online Discussion Forums\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a11bc09b64285029\">>>","url":"https://www.cfd-online.com/Forums/openfoam-meshing/178909-snappyhexmesh-parallel-run-face-ordering-problem.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"84c24ccea73f9ac9\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHi all, wondering if you can help me with a problem that has been plaguing my simulations for weeks now!! I have no problem setting up and running my\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"84c24ccea73f9ac9\">>>","siteName":"www.cfd-online.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"871aab2efe751f8a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\npde - Algorithm for face based data-structure - CFD - Computational Science Stack Exchange\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"871aab2efe751f8a\">>>","url":"https://scicomp.stackexchange.com/questions/25431/algorithm-for-face-based-data-structure-cfd","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fc5c7d27bffe08ae\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n$\\begingroup$ I know a code that has done a very similar procedure which collects face data from unstructured mesh output from GMSH (gmsh.info). Here is it: dolfyn.net/dolfyn/downloads_en.html Authors wrote the code in Fortran90 and the code is quite simple to be read.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fc5c7d27bffe08ae\">>>","siteName":"scicomp.stackexchange.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dcb355435c164586\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGenerating Multibillion Element Unstructured Meshes on Distributed Memory Parallel Machines - Soner - 2015 - Scientific Programming - Wiley Online Library\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dcb355435c164586\">>>","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2015/437480","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4806b3ed36c5b629\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFinally, we note that when new mesh entities are created in parallel, duplicate entities on the partition boundaries result which need to be matched. A somewhat similar problem occurs in the OpenFOAM reconstructParMesh utility. We quote Piscaglia et al. [18] on this problem: “ … the point, face and cell processor addressing lists, generated at decomposition time, are no longer valid once topological changes have occurred.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4806b3ed36c5b629\">>>","published":"May 26, 2015","siteName":"onlinelibrary.wiley.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"098a195305e3f8bd\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMassively Parallel CFD Simulation Software: CCFD Development and Optimization Based on Sunway TaihuLight - Liu - 2020 - Scientific Programming - Wiley Online Library\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"098a195305e3f8bd\">>>","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2020/8847481","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"31f28911c53e2294\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nUsing the multiblock structure grid parallel technology of domain decomposition, we design the parallel software framework, software process, parallel data structure, and communication mode of parallel CFD solver software. The designed CCFD can be used for large-scale mega-core parallel computing tasks and has good parallel expansion ability and parallel efficiency. The overdecomposition load balancing strategy for CCFD guarantees the load balancing performance of computing and communication. The use of a mesh sequencing method based on multigrid technology can accelerate the iterative convergence speed.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"31f28911c53e2294\">>>","published":"July 22, 2020","siteName":"onlinelibrary.wiley.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9ca3bc6aa8b86726\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nCoherent mesh representation for parallel I/O of unstructured polyhedral meshes | The Journal of Supercomputing | Springer Nature Link\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9ca3bc6aa8b86726\">>>","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11227-024-06051-7","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"00e7490baf66035b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>This paper presents a new mesh data layout for parallel I/O of linear unstructured polyhedral meshes</strong>. The new mesh representation infers coherence across entities of different topological dimensions, i.e., grid cells, faces, and points.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"00e7490baf66035b\">>>","published":"April 9, 2024","siteName":"link.springer.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"537fe6dfa0d430a2\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nData Structures and Mesh Processing in Parallel CFD Project GIMM ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"537fe6dfa0d430a2\">>>","url":"https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/35010120.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f0446a452ab90305\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe world’s largest collection of open access research papers\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f0446a452ab90305\">>>","siteName":"core.ac.uk"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f646b0c08c52046c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nfluid dynamics - What are some good data-types for unstructured cell-centered FVM CFD code? - Computational Science Stack Exchange\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f646b0c08c52046c\">>>","url":"https://scicomp.stackexchange.com/questions/4733/what-are-some-good-data-types-for-unstructured-cell-centered-fvm-cfd-code","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"578f6742fdc1ccf1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOne example that I encountered (in dolfyn cfd code) goes like this (I&#x27;ll show relevant segment) <strong>\\begin{listing} do ip=1,Ncel ... do j=1,NFaces(ip) k = CFace(ip,j) ipp = Face(k)�ll1 inn = Face(k)�ll2 if( inn &gt; 0 )then ! internal \\end{listing}</strong> So ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"578f6742fdc1ccf1\">>>","siteName":"scicomp.stackexchange.com"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1772620004135}}
{"type":"message","id":"a11a5399","parentId":"5af750a1","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:51.575Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good progress. Now let me search for more specific topics related to the three problems:","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-bd297559f34d423d84612b48f2617160","name":"web_search","arguments":{"count":10,"query":"MPI neighborhood collectives sparse alltoall optimization MPI-3.0"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-6a092174e93844ab936adf8de249b904","name":"web_search","arguments":{"count":10,"query":"ParMETIS elmdist distribution load imbalance partition weights"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-c0e18cf0f3184508916523af893d6558","name":"web_search","arguments":{"count":10,"query":"face hashing mesh connectivity integer ID vs string performance"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":2916,"output":115,"cacheRead":33855,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":36886,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1772620004137}}
{"type":"message","id":"fe28d19f","parentId":"a11a5399","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:52.824Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-bd297559f34d423d84612b48f2617160","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"MPI neighborhood collectives sparse alltoall optimization MPI-3.0\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1244,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5ae689d001cc5178\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOptimization Principles for Collective Neighborhood Communications\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5ae689d001cc5178\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-schneider-neighbor-colls.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd0c475dcc8f31c7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nfor example, the global alltoall of a Fast Fourier Transform, or the allreduce convergence check of many Monte Carlo ... A. Neighborhood Collectives in MPI-3.0 · The concept of neighborhood collectives has initially been · motivated under the name sparse collectives in [6], [7]. The\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd0c475dcc8f31c7\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"htor.inf.ethz.ch\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"826c7194a0674ca8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSparse Collective Operations for MPI Torsten Hoeﬂer Open Systems Lab\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"826c7194a0674ca8\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-sparsecolls.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"99beaa02297f8420\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe user has to create a (local) neighborhood at · each process and either create a topological communicator or · attach it to a neighbor operation. It was already shown in [16] that such abstractly deﬁned collective operations can simplify ... D. Scheduling communication and other optimizations · For the regular, sparse all-to-all collective, the experiments\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"99beaa02297f8420\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"htor.inf.ethz.ch\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8423f7302ba9aceb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n(PDF) Sparse collective operations for MPI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8423f7302ba9aceb\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220949851_Sparse_collective_operations_for_MPI\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"061321e5ce2fc920\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe common optimization for eliminating diagonal communication of the y regions by piggy backing could easily be done transparently to the user with a good implementation of MPI Neighbor alltoallw. ... Two dimensional mesh or torus (wrap-around edges not shown). The local neighborhood of each process indicated with heavy lines consists of the same 4 source and target processes. ... Running times for the two sparse all-to-all implementations on an NEC SX-8 node with 8 processes.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"061321e5ce2fc920\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"May 1, 2009\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fa4ac406e01f1485\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSparse collective operations for MPI | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fa4ac406e01f1485\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5160935/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c6c86e7ccbe1a9c4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>We discuss issues in designing sparse (nearest neighbor) collective operations for communication and reduction operations in small neighborhoods for the message passing interface (MPI). We propose three such operations, namely a sparse gather operation, a sparse all-to-all, and a sparse reduction operation in both regular and irregular (vector) variants</strong>...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c6c86e7ccbe1a9c4\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"ieeexplore.ieee.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2a06e1f394e553c2\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSparse collective operations for MPI | Proceedings of the 2009 IEEE International Symposium on Parallel&Distributed Processing\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2a06e1f394e553c2\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/IPDPS.2009.5160935\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e2c6590772c0d37f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>We discuss issues in designing sparse (nearest neighbor) collective operations for communication and reduction operations in small neighborhoods for the Message Passing Interface (MPI).</strong>We propose three such operations, namely a sparse gather operation, a sparse all-to-all, and a sparse reduction ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e2c6590772c0d37f\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"dl.acm.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c053f51fc50d27df\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n[mpi3-coll] Reduction in neighborhood collectives, persistence?\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c053f51fc50d27df\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://lists.mpi-forum.org/pipermail/mpiwg-coll/2011-November/000551.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e30f3f4ded6e51c4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThis page looks pretty &gt; &gt; bare: [2]https://svn.mpi-forum.org/trac/mpi-forum-web/wiki/PersColl &gt; &gt; We would be especially interested in persistent versions of the &gt; &gt; neighborhood &quot;v&quot; and &quot;w&quot; variants because it lets us build a persistent &gt; &gt; handle so that the MPI implementation can determine all message sizes &gt; once &gt; &gt; in setup instead of needing to rebuild it in each call (of which there &gt; may &gt; &gt; be thousands or millions). In my opinion, not offering it is an implicit &gt; &gt; declaration that you think there is no setup that can be amortized &gt; across &gt; &gt; calls. This seems unlikely to me in the case of neighbor collectives.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e30f3f4ded6e51c4\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"lists.mpi-forum.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"951c75ac4178c741\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA Preview of MPI 3.0: The Shape of Things to Come Manjunath Gorentla Venkata\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"951c75ac4178c741\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.olcf.ornl.gov/wp-content/uploads/2012/08/mpi-part-2-manju.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3f53eac2676c7d3b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHoefler et al. : Implemention and Performance Analysis of Non-Blocking Collective Operations for MPI ... Gorentla et al. : Exploring the All-To-All Collective Optimization Space with ConnectX CORE-Direct\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3f53eac2676c7d3b\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.olcf.ornl.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a9707af61cf93655\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOptimizing Irregular Communication with Neighborhood Collectives and Locality-Aware Parallelism (Conference) | OSTI.GOV\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a9707af61cf93655\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/2205620\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9afd5ee99c9bf3e8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHere, the authors analyze three implementations of persistent neighborhood collectives for Alltoallv: an unoptimized wrapper of standard point-to-point communication, and two locality-aware aggregating methods. The second locality-aware implementation exposes an non-standard interface to perform additional optimization, and the authors present the additional 0.07x speedup from the extended interface. All optimizations are available in an open-source codebase, MPI Advance, which sits on top of MPI, allowing for optimizations to be added into existing codebases regardless of the system MPI install.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9afd5ee99c9bf3e8\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"November 12, 2023\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.osti.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"aaa92c36621656ed\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOptimization of Applications with Non-blocking Neighborhood Collectives via Multisends on the Blue Gene/P Supercomputer - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"aaa92c36621656ed\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3111918/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"85c6be2d8e196560\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe MPI_Alltoallv collective operation on BG/P is implemented on top of DCMF_Manytomany by setting rIndex to the relative rank of the sender in the communicator. The DCMF_Manytomany call is more general than MPI_Alltoallv as it can efficiently implement sparse collective operations where each processor sends data to a small subset of processors in the communicator.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"85c6be2d8e196560\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"54b542c9921d0f72\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n[1606.07676] Message-Combining Algorithms for Isomorphic, Sparse Collective Communication\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"54b542c9921d0f72\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.07676\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"02c8d92566612b4a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe give schemes for \\\\emph{isomorphic \\\\alltoall} and \\\\emph{\\\\allgather} communication that reduce the number of communication rounds and thereby the communication latency from $s$ to at most $Nd$, for neighborhoods consisting of $s$ processes with the (small) factor $N$ depending on the structure of the neighborhood and the capabilities of the communication system. Using these schedules, we give \\\\emph{zero-copy implementations} of the isomorphic collectives using MPI and its derived datatypes to eliminate explicit, process-local copy operations.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"02c8d92566612b4a\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"June 24, 2016\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"details":{"query":"MPI neighborhood collectives sparse alltoall optimization MPI-3.0","provider":"brave","count":10,"tookMs":1244,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"web_search","provider":"brave","wrapped":true},"results":[{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5ae689d001cc5178\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOptimization Principles for Collective Neighborhood Communications\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5ae689d001cc5178\">>>","url":"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-schneider-neighbor-colls.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bd0c475dcc8f31c7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nfor example, the global alltoall of a Fast Fourier Transform, or the allreduce convergence check of many Monte Carlo ... A. Neighborhood Collectives in MPI-3.0 · The concept of neighborhood collectives has initially been · motivated under the name sparse collectives in [6], [7]. The\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bd0c475dcc8f31c7\">>>","siteName":"htor.inf.ethz.ch"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"826c7194a0674ca8\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSparse Collective Operations for MPI Torsten Hoeﬂer Open Systems Lab\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"826c7194a0674ca8\">>>","url":"https://htor.inf.ethz.ch/publications/img/hoefler-sparsecolls.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"99beaa02297f8420\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe user has to create a (local) neighborhood at · each process and either create a topological communicator or · attach it to a neighbor operation. It was already shown in [16] that such abstractly deﬁned collective operations can simplify ... D. Scheduling communication and other optimizations · For the regular, sparse all-to-all collective, the experiments\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"99beaa02297f8420\">>>","siteName":"htor.inf.ethz.ch"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8423f7302ba9aceb\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n(PDF) Sparse collective operations for MPI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8423f7302ba9aceb\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220949851_Sparse_collective_operations_for_MPI","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"061321e5ce2fc920\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe common optimization for eliminating diagonal communication of the y regions by piggy backing could easily be done transparently to the user with a good implementation of MPI Neighbor alltoallw. ... Two dimensional mesh or torus (wrap-around edges not shown). The local neighborhood of each process indicated with heavy lines consists of the same 4 source and target processes. ... Running times for the two sparse all-to-all implementations on an NEC SX-8 node with 8 processes.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"061321e5ce2fc920\">>>","published":"May 1, 2009","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fa4ac406e01f1485\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSparse collective operations for MPI | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fa4ac406e01f1485\">>>","url":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5160935/","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c6c86e7ccbe1a9c4\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>We discuss issues in designing sparse (nearest neighbor) collective operations for communication and reduction operations in small neighborhoods for the message passing interface (MPI). We propose three such operations, namely a sparse gather operation, a sparse all-to-all, and a sparse reduction operation in both regular and irregular (vector) variants</strong>...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c6c86e7ccbe1a9c4\">>>","siteName":"ieeexplore.ieee.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2a06e1f394e553c2\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSparse collective operations for MPI | Proceedings of the 2009 IEEE International Symposium on Parallel&Distributed Processing\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2a06e1f394e553c2\">>>","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/IPDPS.2009.5160935","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e2c6590772c0d37f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>We discuss issues in designing sparse (nearest neighbor) collective operations for communication and reduction operations in small neighborhoods for the Message Passing Interface (MPI).</strong>We propose three such operations, namely a sparse gather operation, a sparse all-to-all, and a sparse reduction ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e2c6590772c0d37f\">>>","siteName":"dl.acm.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c053f51fc50d27df\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n[mpi3-coll] Reduction in neighborhood collectives, persistence?\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c053f51fc50d27df\">>>","url":"https://lists.mpi-forum.org/pipermail/mpiwg-coll/2011-November/000551.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e30f3f4ded6e51c4\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThis page looks pretty &gt; &gt; bare: [2]https://svn.mpi-forum.org/trac/mpi-forum-web/wiki/PersColl &gt; &gt; We would be especially interested in persistent versions of the &gt; &gt; neighborhood &quot;v&quot; and &quot;w&quot; variants because it lets us build a persistent &gt; &gt; handle so that the MPI implementation can determine all message sizes &gt; once &gt; &gt; in setup instead of needing to rebuild it in each call (of which there &gt; may &gt; &gt; be thousands or millions). In my opinion, not offering it is an implicit &gt; &gt; declaration that you think there is no setup that can be amortized &gt; across &gt; &gt; calls. This seems unlikely to me in the case of neighbor collectives.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e30f3f4ded6e51c4\">>>","siteName":"lists.mpi-forum.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"951c75ac4178c741\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nA Preview of MPI 3.0: The Shape of Things to Come Manjunath Gorentla Venkata\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"951c75ac4178c741\">>>","url":"https://www.olcf.ornl.gov/wp-content/uploads/2012/08/mpi-part-2-manju.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3f53eac2676c7d3b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHoefler et al. : Implemention and Performance Analysis of Non-Blocking Collective Operations for MPI ... Gorentla et al. : Exploring the All-To-All Collective Optimization Space with ConnectX CORE-Direct\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3f53eac2676c7d3b\">>>","siteName":"www.olcf.ornl.gov"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a9707af61cf93655\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOptimizing Irregular Communication with Neighborhood Collectives and Locality-Aware Parallelism (Conference) | OSTI.GOV\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a9707af61cf93655\">>>","url":"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/2205620","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9afd5ee99c9bf3e8\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHere, the authors analyze three implementations of persistent neighborhood collectives for Alltoallv: an unoptimized wrapper of standard point-to-point communication, and two locality-aware aggregating methods. The second locality-aware implementation exposes an non-standard interface to perform additional optimization, and the authors present the additional 0.07x speedup from the extended interface. All optimizations are available in an open-source codebase, MPI Advance, which sits on top of MPI, allowing for optimizations to be added into existing codebases regardless of the system MPI install.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9afd5ee99c9bf3e8\">>>","published":"November 12, 2023","siteName":"www.osti.gov"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"aaa92c36621656ed\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOptimization of Applications with Non-blocking Neighborhood Collectives via Multisends on the Blue Gene/P Supercomputer - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"aaa92c36621656ed\">>>","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3111918/","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"85c6be2d8e196560\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe MPI_Alltoallv collective operation on BG/P is implemented on top of DCMF_Manytomany by setting rIndex to the relative rank of the sender in the communicator. The DCMF_Manytomany call is more general than MPI_Alltoallv as it can efficiently implement sparse collective operations where each processor sends data to a small subset of processors in the communicator.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"85c6be2d8e196560\">>>","siteName":"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"54b542c9921d0f72\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n[1606.07676] Message-Combining Algorithms for Isomorphic, Sparse Collective Communication\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"54b542c9921d0f72\">>>","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.07676","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"02c8d92566612b4a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe give schemes for \\emph{isomorphic \\alltoall} and \\emph{\\allgather} communication that reduce the number of communication rounds and thereby the communication latency from $s$ to at most $Nd$, for neighborhoods consisting of $s$ processes with the (small) factor $N$ depending on the structure of the neighborhood and the capabilities of the communication system. Using these schedules, we give \\emph{zero-copy implementations} of the isomorphic collectives using MPI and its derived datatypes to eliminate explicit, process-local copy operations.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"02c8d92566612b4a\">>>","published":"June 24, 2016","siteName":"arxiv.org"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1772620012821}}
{"type":"message","id":"5f04eb1e","parentId":"fe28d19f","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:54.097Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-6a092174e93844ab936adf8de249b904","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"ParMETIS elmdist distribution load imbalance partition weights\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1268,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8e7ae856f9f79ade\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n(PDF) Parmetis: Parallel graph partitioning and sparse matrix ordering library\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8e7ae856f9f79ade\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238705993_Parmetis_Parallel_graph_partitioning_and_sparse_matrix_ordering_library\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ed0c67d07af38914\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n... <strong>This routine is used to compute a k-way partitioning of a mesh on pprocessors</strong>. The mesh can contain elements ... elmdist This array describes how the elements of the mesh are distributed among the processors.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ed0c67d07af38914\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 1, 1997\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"21dfe947cdb0aad8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nZoltan User's Guide: ParMETIS Interface\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"21dfe947cdb0aad8\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://sandialabs.github.io/Zoltan/ug_html/ug_alg_parmetis.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8ccbf24dd927d6ec\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe give only a brief summary of ... see the ParMETIS documentation. The methods fall into three categories: Part* - Perform graph partitioning without consideration of the initial distribution. (LB_APPROACH=partition) AdaptiveRepart - Incremental algorithms with small migration cost. (LB_APPROACH=repartition) Refine* - Refines a given partition (balance). Can be applied multiple times to reduce the communication cost (cut weight) if ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8ccbf24dd927d6ec\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"sandialabs.github.io\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8d3255a9436d93e6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEvaluation of Graph Partitioning Algorithms for Load ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8d3255a9436d93e6\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://home.cit.tum.de/~schnelle/publications/bachelorsthesis-informatics-final.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"89616e86c6ffe2f0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe size of this array is therefore the · combined length of all local adjacency lists. In our case, it is less or equal than 4n, ... <strong>Partition weights are supplied as an array of double or float which sums up to 1.</strong> The · weights may diﬀer for each diﬀerent vertex weight. There is also ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"89616e86c6ffe2f0\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"home.cit.tum.de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"63cc9c58dc005528\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n32. — Mesh Partitioning and Load Balancing — 32. 32-1 Sabina Rips\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"63cc9c58dc005528\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://fs.hlrs.de/projects/par/par_prog_ws/pdf/lastverteilung_rips_engl.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b4f34100a03faf18\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n• dynamic load distribution · • serial and parallel · • http://www.gre.ac.uk/jostle · • HLRS: http://www.hlrs.de/organization/par/ services/tools/loadbalancer/jostle.html · • Metis: (George Karypis, University of Minnesota) • multilevel partitioning algorithms · • initial partitioning · • serial · • (parMetis: parallel version) • http://www.cs.umn.edu/~karypis/metis/metis.html ·\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b4f34100a03faf18\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"fs.hlrs.de\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6e3e2c3e38f83e00\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nUsing weight with parmetis partitioner - mesh - FEniCS Project\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6e3e2c3e38f83e00\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://fenicsproject.discourse.group/t/using-weight-with-parmetis-partitioner/17639\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"770f2a138327dd1e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHello, In version 0.9 of C++ Dolfinx, I could not find a way to perform load balancing on a mesh using weights per cell, as proposed by the Parmetis API. I just want to check that I didn’t miss the right solution, as I did what I wanted anyway with the following C++ code : header CPP with a partial copy of partitioners.cpp (only the modified part is shown). and before calling parmetis which can be used as follow: // weights computed above the way you want const auto partitioner = ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"770f2a138327dd1e\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"May 20, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"fenicsproject.discourse.group\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"792c99abdfa5e3cf\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGitHub - KarypisLab/ParMETIS: ParMETIS - Parallel Graph Partitioning and Fill-reducing Matrix Ordering\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"792c99abdfa5e3cf\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/KarypisLab/ParMETIS\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4ce9c9ba03e1901c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe algorithms implemented in ParMETIS are based on the multilevel recursive-bisection, multilevel k-way, and multi-constraint partitioning schemes developed in our lab.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4ce9c9ba03e1901c\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"github.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d6dadb2896ae95a9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDynamic Load Balancing of Plasma and Flow Simulations 1st Gerrett Diamond\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d6dadb2896ae95a9\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://sc18.supercomputing.org/proceedings/workshops/workshop_files/ws_lasalss118s2-file1.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6957fab37c672c6d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nParMETIS and after EnGPar and ParMA are used. Both tools · signiﬁcantly reduce the mesh vertex imbalance. For the 128Ki · and 256Ki cases, both reduce the imbalance to the target 5%. In the 512Ki case, EnGPar reduces the mesh vertex imbalance ... Fig. 4. Vertex imbalance for the initial partitioning and the partitions created\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6957fab37c672c6d\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"sc18.supercomputing.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bcc12b80b75d1bc5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nsrc/dm/partitioner/impls/parmetis/partparmetis.c\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bcc12b80b75d1bc5\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://petsc.org/main/src/dm/partitioner/impls/parmetis/partparmetis.c.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"da8d3cc40a49194f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n+ -petscpartitioner_parmetis_type &lt;string&gt; - ParMETIS partitioning type. Either &quot;kway&quot; or &quot;rb&quot; (recursive bisection) 249: . -petscpartitioner_parmetis_imbalance_ratio &lt;value&gt; - Load imbalance ratio limit 250:\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"da8d3cc40a49194f\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"petsc.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"eb5456b6a886ddec\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nc++ - Partitioning graph using ParMETIS - Stack Overflow\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"eb5456b6a886ddec\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/54555593/partitioning-graph-using-parmetis\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d230f30c754fce1a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nParMETIS_V3_RefineKway(vtxdist,xadj,adjncy, vwgt, adjwgt, &amp;wgtflag, &amp;numflag, ncon, nparts, tpwgts, ubvec, options, &amp;edgecut, part, &amp;comm);\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d230f30c754fce1a\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"stackoverflow.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"28359092b0e29320\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA brief overview of the functionality provided by PARMETIS. The shaded... | Download Scientific Diagram\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"28359092b0e29320\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/A-brief-overview-of-the-functionality-provided-by-PARMETIS-The-shaded-boxes-correspond_fig1_238705993\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"51248f96756cf826\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n... For graph partitioning, to ensure a balanced distribution of training samples and prevent load imbalances among different workers, we <strong>assign node weights based on in-degree of nodes and training masks</strong> when configuring METIS\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"51248f96756cf826\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"details":{"query":"ParMETIS elmdist distribution load imbalance partition weights","provider":"brave","count":10,"tookMs":1268,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"web_search","provider":"brave","wrapped":true},"results":[{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8e7ae856f9f79ade\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n(PDF) Parmetis: Parallel graph partitioning and sparse matrix ordering library\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8e7ae856f9f79ade\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/238705993_Parmetis_Parallel_graph_partitioning_and_sparse_matrix_ordering_library","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ed0c67d07af38914\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n... <strong>This routine is used to compute a k-way partitioning of a mesh on pprocessors</strong>. The mesh can contain elements ... elmdist This array describes how the elements of the mesh are distributed among the processors.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ed0c67d07af38914\">>>","published":"January 1, 1997","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"21dfe947cdb0aad8\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nZoltan User's Guide: ParMETIS Interface\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"21dfe947cdb0aad8\">>>","url":"https://sandialabs.github.io/Zoltan/ug_html/ug_alg_parmetis.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8ccbf24dd927d6ec\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe give only a brief summary of ... see the ParMETIS documentation. The methods fall into three categories: Part* - Perform graph partitioning without consideration of the initial distribution. (LB_APPROACH=partition) AdaptiveRepart - Incremental algorithms with small migration cost. (LB_APPROACH=repartition) Refine* - Refines a given partition (balance). Can be applied multiple times to reduce the communication cost (cut weight) if ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8ccbf24dd927d6ec\">>>","siteName":"sandialabs.github.io"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8d3255a9436d93e6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEvaluation of Graph Partitioning Algorithms for Load ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8d3255a9436d93e6\">>>","url":"https://home.cit.tum.de/~schnelle/publications/bachelorsthesis-informatics-final.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"89616e86c6ffe2f0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe size of this array is therefore the · combined length of all local adjacency lists. In our case, it is less or equal than 4n, ... <strong>Partition weights are supplied as an array of double or float which sums up to 1.</strong> The · weights may diﬀer for each diﬀerent vertex weight. There is also ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"89616e86c6ffe2f0\">>>","siteName":"home.cit.tum.de"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"63cc9c58dc005528\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n32. — Mesh Partitioning and Load Balancing — 32. 32-1 Sabina Rips\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"63cc9c58dc005528\">>>","url":"https://fs.hlrs.de/projects/par/par_prog_ws/pdf/lastverteilung_rips_engl.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b4f34100a03faf18\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n• dynamic load distribution · • serial and parallel · • http://www.gre.ac.uk/jostle · • HLRS: http://www.hlrs.de/organization/par/ services/tools/loadbalancer/jostle.html · • Metis: (George Karypis, University of Minnesota) • multilevel partitioning algorithms · • initial partitioning · • serial · • (parMetis: parallel version) • http://www.cs.umn.edu/~karypis/metis/metis.html ·\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b4f34100a03faf18\">>>","siteName":"fs.hlrs.de"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6e3e2c3e38f83e00\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nUsing weight with parmetis partitioner - mesh - FEniCS Project\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6e3e2c3e38f83e00\">>>","url":"https://fenicsproject.discourse.group/t/using-weight-with-parmetis-partitioner/17639","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"770f2a138327dd1e\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHello, In version 0.9 of C++ Dolfinx, I could not find a way to perform load balancing on a mesh using weights per cell, as proposed by the Parmetis API. I just want to check that I didn’t miss the right solution, as I did what I wanted anyway with the following C++ code : header CPP with a partial copy of partitioners.cpp (only the modified part is shown). and before calling parmetis which can be used as follow: // weights computed above the way you want const auto partitioner = ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"770f2a138327dd1e\">>>","published":"May 20, 2025","siteName":"fenicsproject.discourse.group"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"792c99abdfa5e3cf\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGitHub - KarypisLab/ParMETIS: ParMETIS - Parallel Graph Partitioning and Fill-reducing Matrix Ordering\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"792c99abdfa5e3cf\">>>","url":"https://github.com/KarypisLab/ParMETIS","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4ce9c9ba03e1901c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe algorithms implemented in ParMETIS are based on the multilevel recursive-bisection, multilevel k-way, and multi-constraint partitioning schemes developed in our lab.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4ce9c9ba03e1901c\">>>","siteName":"github.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d6dadb2896ae95a9\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDynamic Load Balancing of Plasma and Flow Simulations 1st Gerrett Diamond\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d6dadb2896ae95a9\">>>","url":"https://sc18.supercomputing.org/proceedings/workshops/workshop_files/ws_lasalss118s2-file1.pdf","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6957fab37c672c6d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nParMETIS and after EnGPar and ParMA are used. Both tools · signiﬁcantly reduce the mesh vertex imbalance. For the 128Ki · and 256Ki cases, both reduce the imbalance to the target 5%. In the 512Ki case, EnGPar reduces the mesh vertex imbalance ... Fig. 4. Vertex imbalance for the initial partitioning and the partitions created\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6957fab37c672c6d\">>>","siteName":"sc18.supercomputing.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bcc12b80b75d1bc5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nsrc/dm/partitioner/impls/parmetis/partparmetis.c\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"bcc12b80b75d1bc5\">>>","url":"https://petsc.org/main/src/dm/partitioner/impls/parmetis/partparmetis.c.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"da8d3cc40a49194f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n+ -petscpartitioner_parmetis_type &lt;string&gt; - ParMETIS partitioning type. Either &quot;kway&quot; or &quot;rb&quot; (recursive bisection) 249: . -petscpartitioner_parmetis_imbalance_ratio &lt;value&gt; - Load imbalance ratio limit 250:\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"da8d3cc40a49194f\">>>","siteName":"petsc.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"eb5456b6a886ddec\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nc++ - Partitioning graph using ParMETIS - Stack Overflow\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"eb5456b6a886ddec\">>>","url":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/54555593/partitioning-graph-using-parmetis","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d230f30c754fce1a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nParMETIS_V3_RefineKway(vtxdist,xadj,adjncy, vwgt, adjwgt, &amp;wgtflag, &amp;numflag, ncon, nparts, tpwgts, ubvec, options, &amp;edgecut, part, &amp;comm);\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d230f30c754fce1a\">>>","siteName":"stackoverflow.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"28359092b0e29320\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nA brief overview of the functionality provided by PARMETIS. The shaded... | Download Scientific Diagram\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"28359092b0e29320\">>>","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/A-brief-overview-of-the-functionality-provided-by-PARMETIS-The-shaded-boxes-correspond_fig1_238705993","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"51248f96756cf826\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n... For graph partitioning, to ensure a balanced distribution of training samples and prevent load imbalances among different workers, we <strong>assign node weights based on in-degree of nodes and training masks</strong> when configuring METIS\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"51248f96756cf826\">>>","siteName":"www.researchgate.net"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1772620014095}}
{"type":"message","id":"cf381085","parentId":"5f04eb1e","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:26:56.466Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-c0e18cf0f3184508916523af893d6558","toolName":"web_search","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"face hashing mesh connectivity integer ID vs string performance\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 2364,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ac091b5b033fd6ab\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nperformance - Most efficient way to generate a mesh's string-hash in unity/c#? - Stack Overflow\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ac091b5b033fd6ab\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/58862178/most-efficient-way-to-generate-a-meshs-string-hash-in-unity-c\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7a4537b85048852c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAs part of my current project, I need to generate hashes for meshes in Unity. These are used to later check whether the mesh content has changed. Due to interface considerations, the hash unfortuna...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7a4537b85048852c\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"stackoverflow.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"059176df3df81dde\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nUnity - Manual: General Optimizations\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"059176df3df81dde\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://docs.unity3d.com/560/Documentation/Manual/BestPracticeUnderstandingPerformanceInUnity7.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f1a463c1bff6edb5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nTherefore, whenever using a Set or Get method on an Animator, Material or Shader, <strong>use the integer-valued method instead of the string-valued methods</strong>. The string methods simply perform string hashing and then forward the hashed ID to the integer-valued methods.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f1a463c1bff6edb5\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"docs.unity3d.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"da971d6dcbd8c990\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n[1504.06804] High Speed Hashing for Integers and Strings\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"da971d6dcbd8c990\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.06804\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"09101623bfd3c3d1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThese modern hash functions are <strong>often an order of magnitude faster than those presented in standard text books</strong>. They are also simpler to implement, and hence a clear win in practice, but ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"09101623bfd3c3d1\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"May 9, 2020\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fd15c500b9454592\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nUnity String to Hash: The Most Efficient Way to Generate Mesh String Hashes in C#\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fd15c500b9454592\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://copyprogramming.com/howto/most-efficient-way-to-generate-a-mesh-s-string-hash-in-unity-c\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"23426c806e6becf2\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nUse SHA256 only when you need cryptographic security or persistent cache files where collision vulnerability matters. Q: How much performance improvement can I expect from string hashing optimization? A: <strong>Replacing string comparisons with cached integer hashes typically delivers 1000x faster lookups</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"23426c806e6becf2\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"November 16, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"copyprogramming.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"658ddf6ae156e448\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nhashtable - Which is faster, String or Integer as hashkey in Java? - Stack Overflow\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"658ddf6ae156e448\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/15632300/which-is-faster-string-or-integer-as-hashkey-in-java\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6652e6b12ae9d398\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>String&#x27;s hashcode is cached so it should not make much difference</strong>. The problem is probably somewhere else... You should profile your code to find the bottleneck. Note: in a single threaded situation, HashMap will perform slightly better than ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6652e6b12ae9d398\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"stackoverflow.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"098fe2bc4b969f0b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nhash - Hashes vs Numeric id's - Stack Overflow\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"098fe2bc4b969f0b\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/196726/hashes-vs-numeric-ids\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"20a22af4feac0157\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nYeah, I don&#x27;t think you&#x27;re looking for a hash - you&#x27;re more likely looking for a Guid.If you&#x27;re on the .Net platform, try System.Guid. However, the most important reason not to use a Guid is for performance. Doing database joins and lookups on (long) strings is very suboptimal.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"20a22af4feac0157\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"stackoverflow.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3d08074f0763b8ac\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nc++ - String hashing design - Game Development Stack Exchange\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3d08074f0763b8ac\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://gamedev.stackexchange.com/questions/187674/string-hashing-design\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e56e8852cd614d33\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWhen you later need the additional performance you can then change the implementation to one that uses integer comparisons. ... \\\\$\\\\begingroup\\\\$ Many thanks for the answer and for the unsolicited advice. I had forgotten that only the int data should be available in the &#x27;retail&#x27; version. I&#x27;ll look into FName via the link you gave. Regarding the level of complexity int ids introduce, is there anything specific I should be aware of?\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e56e8852cd614d33\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"gamedev.stackexchange.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"56d2a5e21b47a6ee\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nsecurity - Which hashing algorithm is best for uniqueness and speed? - Software Engineering Stack Exchange\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"56d2a5e21b47a6ee\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/questions/49550/which-hashing-algorithm-is-best-for-uniqueness-and-speed\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d2af0c0d64af08bf\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nPaul Hsieh once made fast hash. He lists source code and explanations. But it was already beaten. :) ... Save this answer. Show activity on this post. ... using int arithmetic, where s[i] is the i​-th character of the string, n is the length of the string, and ^ indicates exponentiation.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d2af0c0d64af08bf\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"softwareengineering.stackexchange.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"81bc91a2af88f24d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHashing - The Rust Performance Book\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"81bc91a2af88f24d\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://nnethercote.github.io/perf-book/hashing.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"12198c8c0357f879\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nrustc-hash provides FxHashSet and FxHashMap types that are drop-in replacements for HashSet and HashMap. Its hashing algorithm is <strong>low-quality but very fast, especially for integer keys, and has been found to out-perform all other hash algorithms within rustc</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"12198c8c0357f879\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"nnethercote.github.io\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"28694b1c7eb5712b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHashing\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"28694b1c7eb5712b\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://catlikecoding.com/unity/tutorials/pseudorandom-noise/hashing/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0d9ac436f320e5e3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>The int type represents a signed integer, which has a special sign bit</strong>. The uint type is unsigned and thus doesn&#x27;t have a special sign bit. All bits of uint values are treated the same way.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0d9ac436f320e5e3\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"May 23, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"catlikecoding.com\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}],"details":{"query":"face hashing mesh connectivity integer ID vs string performance","provider":"brave","count":10,"tookMs":2364,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"web_search","provider":"brave","wrapped":true},"results":[{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ac091b5b033fd6ab\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nperformance - Most efficient way to generate a mesh's string-hash in unity/c#? - Stack Overflow\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ac091b5b033fd6ab\">>>","url":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/58862178/most-efficient-way-to-generate-a-meshs-string-hash-in-unity-c","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7a4537b85048852c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nAs part of my current project, I need to generate hashes for meshes in Unity. These are used to later check whether the mesh content has changed. Due to interface considerations, the hash unfortuna...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7a4537b85048852c\">>>","siteName":"stackoverflow.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"059176df3df81dde\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nUnity - Manual: General Optimizations\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"059176df3df81dde\">>>","url":"https://docs.unity3d.com/560/Documentation/Manual/BestPracticeUnderstandingPerformanceInUnity7.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f1a463c1bff6edb5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nTherefore, whenever using a Set or Get method on an Animator, Material or Shader, <strong>use the integer-valued method instead of the string-valued methods</strong>. The string methods simply perform string hashing and then forward the hashed ID to the integer-valued methods.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f1a463c1bff6edb5\">>>","siteName":"docs.unity3d.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"da971d6dcbd8c990\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n[1504.06804] High Speed Hashing for Integers and Strings\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"da971d6dcbd8c990\">>>","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.06804","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"09101623bfd3c3d1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThese modern hash functions are <strong>often an order of magnitude faster than those presented in standard text books</strong>. They are also simpler to implement, and hence a clear win in practice, but ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"09101623bfd3c3d1\">>>","published":"May 9, 2020","siteName":"arxiv.org"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fd15c500b9454592\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nUnity String to Hash: The Most Efficient Way to Generate Mesh String Hashes in C#\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fd15c500b9454592\">>>","url":"https://copyprogramming.com/howto/most-efficient-way-to-generate-a-mesh-s-string-hash-in-unity-c","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"23426c806e6becf2\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nUse SHA256 only when you need cryptographic security or persistent cache files where collision vulnerability matters. Q: How much performance improvement can I expect from string hashing optimization? A: <strong>Replacing string comparisons with cached integer hashes typically delivers 1000x faster lookups</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"23426c806e6becf2\">>>","published":"November 16, 2025","siteName":"copyprogramming.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"658ddf6ae156e448\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nhashtable - Which is faster, String or Integer as hashkey in Java? - Stack Overflow\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"658ddf6ae156e448\">>>","url":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/15632300/which-is-faster-string-or-integer-as-hashkey-in-java","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6652e6b12ae9d398\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>String&#x27;s hashcode is cached so it should not make much difference</strong>. The problem is probably somewhere else... You should profile your code to find the bottleneck. Note: in a single threaded situation, HashMap will perform slightly better than ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6652e6b12ae9d398\">>>","siteName":"stackoverflow.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"098fe2bc4b969f0b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nhash - Hashes vs Numeric id's - Stack Overflow\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"098fe2bc4b969f0b\">>>","url":"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/196726/hashes-vs-numeric-ids","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"20a22af4feac0157\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nYeah, I don&#x27;t think you&#x27;re looking for a hash - you&#x27;re more likely looking for a Guid.If you&#x27;re on the .Net platform, try System.Guid. However, the most important reason not to use a Guid is for performance. Doing database joins and lookups on (long) strings is very suboptimal.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"20a22af4feac0157\">>>","siteName":"stackoverflow.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3d08074f0763b8ac\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nc++ - String hashing design - Game Development Stack Exchange\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3d08074f0763b8ac\">>>","url":"https://gamedev.stackexchange.com/questions/187674/string-hashing-design","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e56e8852cd614d33\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWhen you later need the additional performance you can then change the implementation to one that uses integer comparisons. ... \\$\\begingroup\\$ Many thanks for the answer and for the unsolicited advice. I had forgotten that only the int data should be available in the &#x27;retail&#x27; version. I&#x27;ll look into FName via the link you gave. Regarding the level of complexity int ids introduce, is there anything specific I should be aware of?\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e56e8852cd614d33\">>>","siteName":"gamedev.stackexchange.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"56d2a5e21b47a6ee\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nsecurity - Which hashing algorithm is best for uniqueness and speed? - Software Engineering Stack Exchange\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"56d2a5e21b47a6ee\">>>","url":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/questions/49550/which-hashing-algorithm-is-best-for-uniqueness-and-speed","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d2af0c0d64af08bf\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nPaul Hsieh once made fast hash. He lists source code and explanations. But it was already beaten. :) ... Save this answer. Show activity on this post. ... using int arithmetic, where s[i] is the i​-th character of the string, n is the length of the string, and ^ indicates exponentiation.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d2af0c0d64af08bf\">>>","siteName":"softwareengineering.stackexchange.com"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"81bc91a2af88f24d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHashing - The Rust Performance Book\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"81bc91a2af88f24d\">>>","url":"https://nnethercote.github.io/perf-book/hashing.html","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"12198c8c0357f879\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nrustc-hash provides FxHashSet and FxHashMap types that are drop-in replacements for HashSet and HashMap. Its hashing algorithm is <strong>low-quality but very fast, especially for integer keys, and has been found to out-perform all other hash algorithms within rustc</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"12198c8c0357f879\">>>","siteName":"nnethercote.github.io"},{"title":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"28694b1c7eb5712b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHashing\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"28694b1c7eb5712b\">>>","url":"https://catlikecoding.com/unity/tutorials/pseudorandom-noise/hashing/","description":"\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0d9ac436f320e5e3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>The int type represents a signed integer, which has a special sign bit</strong>. The uint type is unsigned and thus doesn&#x27;t have a special sign bit. All bits of uint values are treated the same way.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0d9ac436f320e5e3\">>>","published":"May 23, 2021","siteName":"catlikecoding.com"}]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1772620016463}}
{"type":"message","id":"6081899d","parentId":"cf381085","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:27:03.806Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have collected enough information. Now let me analyze the code and write the research report.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"让我进一步分析代码中的关键函数并查看相关配置："},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-6ffaebad495f44c8b8652c8abccf6619","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"/home/one/projects/cgnsMeshCutter/src/cgFile.cpp"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":8293,"output":74,"cacheRead":36671,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":45038,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1772620016466}}
{"type":"message","id":"d1117884","parentId":"6081899d","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:27:03.814Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"tool-6ffaebad495f44c8b8652c8abccf6619","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#include \"cgFile.h\"\n#include \"format.h\"\n\n#include <cgns_io.h>\n\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <fstream>\n#include <functional>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <numeric>\n#include <sstream>\n\nstatic std::unordered_map<DataType_t, std::function<void(void *&, const size_t len)>> New{{DataType_t::Integer, [](void *&ptr, const size_t len) { ptr = new int[len]; }}, {DataType_t::LongInteger, [](void *&ptr, const size_t len) { ptr = new long[len]; }}};\n\nstatic std::unordered_map<DataType_t, std::function<void(void *&)>> Delete{{DataType_t::Integer,\n                                                                            [](void *&ptr)\n                                                                            {\n                                                                                int *tmp = ((int *)ptr);\n                                                                                delete[] tmp;\n                                                                                ptr = nullptr;\n                                                                            }},\n                                                                           {DataType_t::LongInteger, [](void *&ptr)\n                                                                            {\n                                                                                long *tmp = ((long *)ptr);\n                                                                                delete[] tmp;\n                                                                                ptr = nullptr;\n                                                                            }}};\n\nstatic std::unordered_map<DataType_t, std::function<long(void *&, const size_t id)>> Get{{DataType_t::Integer, [](void *&ptr, const size_t id) { return ((int *)ptr)[id]; }}, {DataType_t::LongInteger, [](void *&ptr, const size_t id) { return ((long *)ptr)[id]; }}};\n\nstatic std::unordered_map<DataType_t, std::function<void(void *&, const size_t, void *&, const size_t)>> Copy{{DataType_t::Integer, [](void *&dst, const size_t dstId, void *&src, const size_t srcId) { ((int *)dst)[dstId] = ((int *)src)[srcId]; }},\n                                                                                                              {DataType_t::LongInteger, [](void *&dst, const size_t dstId, void *&src, const size_t srcId) { ((long *)dst)[dstId] = ((long *)src)[srcId]; }}};\n\nstatic std::unordered_map<DataType_t, std::function<long(void *&, const size_t)>> Cast2cgsize_t{{DataType_t::Integer, [](void *&src, const size_t id) -> long { return cgsize_t(((int *)src)[id]); }}, {DataType_t::LongInteger, [](void *&src, const size_t id) -> long { return ((long *)src)[id]; }}};\n\nstatic cgsize_t BLOCK_LEN_EACH_LOAD = 10000;\n\nnamespace MeshCut\n{\nvoid check(bool state, string &&errStr, string &&okStr)\n{\n    if (!state)\n    {\n        if (!errStr.empty()) { std::cerr << (errStr.c_str()); }\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        if (!okStr.empty()) std::cout << (okStr.c_str());\n    }\n}\n\nvoid check(const int runCode, string &&errStr, string &&okStr)\n{\n    if (runCode != CG_OK)\n    {\n        if (!errStr.empty()) { std::cerr << \"ERROR: \" << (errStr.c_str()); }\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        if (!okStr.empty()) std::cout << (okStr.c_str());\n    }\n}\n\n// 各种单元类型每个面的节点在单元内部的编号。\n// 由于面的法向量具有方向性，这些数据在计算相关向量时需要使用。\nconst unordered_map<ElementType_t, vector<vector<int>>> CGFile::nodeIdToBuildFaceInCell = {{ElementType_t::TETRA_4, {{0, 2, 1}, {0, 1, 3}, {1, 2, 3}, {2, 0, 3}}},\n                                                                                           {ElementType_t::HEXA_8, {{0, 3, 2, 1}, {0, 1, 5, 4}, {1, 2, 6, 5}, {2, 3, 7, 6}, {0, 4, 7, 3}, {4, 5, 6, 7}}},\n                                                                                           {ElementType_t::PENTA_6, {{0, 2, 1}, {0, 1, 4, 3}, {1, 2, 5, 4}, {2, 0, 3, 5}, {3, 4, 5}}},\n                                                                                           {ElementType_t::PYRA_5, {{0, 3, 2, 1}, {0, 1, 4}, {1, 2, 4}, {2, 3, 4}, {3, 0, 4}}}};\n\nCGFile::CellLoader::CellLoader(Section &curS, const int fp, const cgsize_t lowerBd, const cgsize_t upperBd) : curS(curS), fp(fp)\n{\n    lowerBound = std::max(lowerBd, curS.start);\n    upperBound = std::min(upperBd, curS.end);\n    blockSize = std::min(upperBound - lowerBound + 1, BLOCK_LEN_EACH_LOAD);\n    start = lowerBound - blockSize;\n    loadNextBlock();\n}\n\nbool CGFile::CellLoader::nextCell(vector<cgsize_t> &nodes)\n{\n    if (id >= len && !loadNextBlock()) return false;\n\n    nodes.clear();\n\n    cgsize_t begin, end;\n    if (curS.isMixed())\n    {\n        begin = offset[id] + 1;\n        end = offset[id + 1];\n        flag = data[begin - 1];\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        begin = npe * id;\n        end = begin + npe;\n    }\n\n    for (auto j = begin; j < end; ++j) { nodes.push_back(data[j]); }\n\n    ++id;\n\n    return true;\n}\n\nbool CGFile::CellLoader::loadNextBlock()\n{\n    if (start + blockSize > upperBound) return false;\n\n    cgsize_t curBlockDataSize = 0;\n    start += blockSize;\n    auto end = std::min(start + blockSize - 1, upperBound);\n    len = end - start + 1;\n    cg_ElementPartialSize(fp, 1, 1, curS.id, start, end, &curBlockDataSize);\n\n    vector<cgsize_t>{}.swap(data);\n    if (curS.isMixed())\n    {\n        vector<cgsize_t>{}.swap(offset);\n        data.assign(curBlockDataSize, 0);\n        offset.assign(len + 1, 0);\n        cg_poly_elements_partial_read(fp, 1, 1, curS.id, start, end, data.data(), offset.data(), NULL);\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        cg_npe(curS.cellType, &npe);\n        data.assign(curBlockDataSize, 0);\n        cg_elements_partial_read(fp, 1, 1, curS.id, start, end, data.data(), NULL);\n    }\n\n    ++blockId;\n    id = 0;\n\n    return true;\n}\n\nvoid CGFile::Section::addCell(const cgsize_t id, const vector<cgsize_t> &idList, const cgsize_t flag)\n{\n    data.emplace_back(idList);\n    IDMap.insert({id, IDMap.size()});\n    typeFlag.push_back(flag);\n    offset.push_back(offset.back() + idList.size() + 1);\n}\n\nconst vector<cgsize_t> &CGFile::Section::cell(const int id) const { return data[IDMap.at(id)]; }\n\nvoid CGFile::Section::clear()\n{\n    vector<vector<cgsize_t>>{}.swap(data);\n    vector<cgsize_t>{0}.swap(offset);\n    vector<cgsize_t>{}.swap(typeFlag);\n    map<cgsize_t, cgsize_t>{}.swap(IDMap);\n}\n\ncgsize_t CGFile::Section::flag(const int id) const { return isMixed() ? typeFlag[IDMap.at(id)] : (cgsize_t)cellType; }\n\nbool CGFile::Section::isMixed() const { return (cellType == CGNS_ENUMV(MIXED)); }\n\nvoid CGFile::Section::printToFile(string fname)\n{\n    std::fstream fp(fname, std::ios_base::out);\n    if (!fp.is_open()) std::cout << format(\"Can not open file %s to write\\n\", fname.c_str());\n\n    fp << format(\"Cell -> %d:[%d - %d]\\n\", data.size(), start, end);\n    for (auto i = 0; i < data.size(); ++i)\n    {\n        for (auto it : data[i]) fp << it << ',';\n        if (isMixed()) fp << format(\" => %d,%d\\n\", offset[i], typeFlag[i]);\n    }\n\n    fp.close();\n}\n\nCGFile::Section CGFile::Section::subSection(const cgsize_t beg, const cgsize_t end)\n{\n    Section rst;\n\n    rst.cellType = this->cellType;\n    memcpy(rst.name, this->name, 32);\n    rst.start = beg, rst.end = end;\n    rst.nBdy = this->nBdy;\n\n    for (auto id = beg; id <= end; ++id) { rst.addCell(id, this->cell(id), this->flag(id)); }\n\n    return rst;\n}\n\nCGFile::CGFile(string filename, int mode) : filename_(filename)\n{\n    switch (mode)\n    {\n    case CG_MODE_READ:\n        this->checkFile();\n        // this->loadCGIOInfo();\n        this->loadCoordinateInfo();\n        break;\n    case CG_MODE_WRITE:\n        check(cg_open(filename.c_str(), mode, &fp), format(\"can not open file %s to write\\n\", filename.c_str()));\n        coordname_.push_back(\"CoordinateX\");\n        coordname_.push_back(\"CoordinateY\");\n        coordname_.push_back(\"CoordinateZ\");\n        break;\n    default: break;\n    }\n    isOpen_ = true;\n}\n\nvector<vector<cgsize_t>> CGFile::allFaceInCell(const vector<cgsize_t> &idList, ElementType_t type)\n{\n    check((CGFile::nodeIdToBuildFaceInCell.count(type) != 0), format(\"not supported cell type %d\\n\", type));\n\n    vector<vector<cgsize_t>> rst;\n\n    for (auto nodes : CGFile::nodeIdToBuildFaceInCell.at(type))\n    {\n        vector<cgsize_t> face;\n        for (auto it : nodes) face.push_back(idList[it]);\n        rst.emplace_back(face);\n    }\n\n    return rst;\n}\n\nCGFile::Section &CGFile::addSection()\n{\n    int n = sections_.size();\n    sections_[n] = Section{};\n\n    return sections_[n];\n}\n\nCGFile::Section CGFile::bodySection()\n{\n    Section rst;\n    string name;\n    set<ElementType_t> types;\n    vector<cgsize_t> startFlag;\n\n    for (auto it : bodySectionIDList_)\n    {\n        auto &curS = sections_[it];\n        name += (curS.name + string(\"_\"));\n        startFlag.push_back(curS.start);\n        rst.end = std::max(rst.end, curS.end);\n        types.insert(curS.cellType);\n    }\n    rst.start = *(std::min_element(startFlag.begin(), startFlag.end()));\n    std::memcpy(rst.name, name.c_str(), std::min((size_t)31, name.rfind('_')));\n    rst.cellType = types.size() == 1 ? *types.begin() : ElementType_t::MIXED;\n\n    return rst;\n}\n\nconst vector<int> &CGFile::bodySectionIdList() const { return bodySectionIDList_; }\n\nconst vector<int> &CGFile::bdySectionIdList() const { return bdySectionIDList_; }\n\nvoid CGFile::checkFile()\n{\n    char basename[33], zonename[33];\n    int nbases, nzones, ngrids, ncoords, phys_dim;\n    int filetype;\n    ZoneType_t zoneType;\n    check(cg_is_cgns(filename_.c_str(), &filetype), format(\"%s is not a CGNS file\\n\", filename_.c_str()));\n\n    check((cg_set_file_type(CG_FILE_ADF) == CG_OK && cg_open(filename_.c_str(), CG_MODE_READ, &fp) == CG_OK) || (cg_set_file_type(CG_FILE_HDF5) == CG_OK && cg_open(filename_.c_str(), CG_MODE_READ, &fp) == CG_OK), format(\"can not open file: %s \\n\", filename_.c_str()));\n    isOpen_ = true;\n\n    check((cg_nbases(fp, &nbases), nbases == 1), format(\"now only support one Base, you have %d\\n\", nbases));\n\n    check((cg_base_read(fp, ibase, basename, &cell_dim_, &phys_dim), phys_dim == 3), format(\"now only support 3-Dim mesh, you have %d\\n\", phys_dim));\n\n    check((cg_nzones(fp, ibase, &nzones), nzones == 1), format(\"now only support one Zone, you have %d\\n\", nzones));\n\n    check((cg_zone_type(fp, ibase, izone, &zoneType), zoneType == ZoneType_t::Unstructured), format(\"now only support UnStructured Mesh\\n\"));\n\n    check(cg_zone_read(fp, ibase, izone, zonename, zoneInfo));\n\n    check((cg_ngrids(fp, ibase, izone, &ngrids), ngrids == 1), format(\"now only support single layer Mesh, you have %d\\n\", ngrids));\n\n    int nSection;\n    Section section;\n    check((cg_nsections(fp, ibase, izone, &nSection), nSection > 0), format(\"no section found in file %s\", filename_.c_str()));\n    for (int iSection = 1; iSection <= nSection; ++iSection)\n    {\n        section.id = iSection;\n        int flag;\n        cg_section_read(fp, ibase, izone, iSection, section.name, &section.cellType, &section.start, &section.end, &section.nBdy, &flag);\n        cg_ElementDataSize(fp, ibase, izone, iSection, &section.dataSize);\n        if (this->isBodySection(section))\n            bodySectionIDList_.push_back(iSection);\n        else\n            bdySectionIDList_.push_back(iSection);\n\n        sections_.insert({iSection, section});\n        globalNumber_.insert({iSection, section.end - section.start + 1});\n    }\n\n    coordDataType_ = (this->precision() == 32) ? CGNS_ENUMV(RealSingle) : CGNS_ENUMV(RealDouble);\n}\n\nElementType_t CGFile::CellType(const int nodeCnt, const int dim)\n{\n    switch (dim)\n    {\n    case 2:\n        switch (nodeCnt)\n        {\n        case 3: return ElementType_t::TRI_3; break;\n        case 4: return ElementType_t::QUAD_4; break;\n        default: std::cout << format(\"not supported element type with[dim, nodes] [ %d, %d]\\n\", dim, nodeCnt); break;\n        }\n        break;\n    case 3:\n        switch (nodeCnt)\n        {\n        case 4: return ElementType_t::TETRA_4; break;\n        case 5: return ElementType_t::PYRA_5; break;\n        case 6: return ElementType_t::PENTA_6; break;\n        case 8: return ElementType_t::HEXA_8; break;\n        default: std::cout << format(\"not supported element type with[dim, nodes] [ %d, %d]\\n\", dim, nodeCnt); break;\n        }\n        break;\n    default: break;\n    }\n\n    return ElementTypeNull;\n}\n\nElementType_t CGFile::CellType(const int CGNSCellTypeFalg)\n{\n    auto rst = ElementType_t::ElementTypeNull;\n    switch (CGNSCellTypeFalg)\n    {\n    case 5: rst = ElementType_t::TRI_3; break;\n    case 7: rst = ElementType_t::QUAD_4; break;\n    case 10: rst = ElementType_t::TETRA_4; break;\n    case 12: rst = ElementType_t::PYRA_5; break;\n    case 14: rst = ElementType_t::PENTA_6; break;\n    case 17: rst = ElementType_t::HEXA_8; break;\n    default: std::cout << format(\"unknown element type %d\\n\", static_cast<ElementType_t>(CGNSCellTypeFalg)); break;\n    }\n    return rst;\n}\n\nvoid CGFile::close()\n{\n    if (!isOpen_) return;\n    check(cg_close(fp), \"\", format(\"close file %s\\n\", filename_.c_str()));\n    isOpen_ = false;\n}\n\nbool CGFile::isBodySection(const Section &section)\n{\n    vector<cgsize_t> bodyConn, offset;\n    int cell_dim;\n    switch (section.cellType)\n    {\n    case NODE: cell_dim = 0; break;\n    case BAR_2: cell_dim = 1; break;\n    case TRI_3:\n    case QUAD_4: cell_dim = 2; break;\n    case TETRA_4:\n    case PYRA_5:\n    case PENTA_6:\n    case HEXA_8: cell_dim = 3; break;\n    case MIXED:\n        cgsize_t flag;\n        bodyConn.assign(section.dataSize, 0);\n        offset.assign(section.end - section.start + 1, 0);\n        cg_poly_elements_read(fp, ibase, izone, section.id, bodyConn.data(), offset.data(), &flag);\n        switch (bodyConn[0])\n        {\n        case ElementType_t::TRI_3:\n        case ElementType_t::TRI_6:\n        case ElementType_t::TRI_9:\n        case ElementType_t::TRI_10:\n        case ElementType_t::TRI_12:\n        case ElementType_t::TRI_15:\n        case ElementType_t::QUAD_4:\n        case ElementType_t::QUAD_8:\n        case ElementType_t::QUAD_9:\n        case ElementType_t::QUAD_12:\n        case ElementType_t::QUAD_16:\n        case ElementType_t::QUAD_P4_16:\n        case ElementType_t::QUAD_25: cell_dim = 2; break;\n        default: cell_dim = 3; break;\n        }\n        break;\n    default: std::cerr << (\"Unsupported element type\\n\"); break;\n    }\n    return cell_dim == cell_dim_;\n}\n\nvector<vector<double>> CGFile::loadCoordinate(const cgsize_t rangeMin, const cgsize_t rangeMax)\n{\n    vector<vector<double>> data(coordname_.size(), vector<double>(rangeMax - rangeMin + 1, 0.0));\n    if (coordDataType_ == CGNS_ENUMV(RealDouble))\n    {\n        for (auto i = 0; i < 3; ++i)\n        {\n            auto coordname = coordname_[i].c_str();\n            check(cg_coord_read(fp, ibase, izone, coordname, coordDataType_, &rangeMin, &rangeMax, data[i].data()), format(\"read %s\\n\", coordname));\n        }\n    }\n    else if (coordDataType_ == CGNS_ENUMV(RealSingle))\n    {\n        for (auto i = 0; i < 3; ++i)\n        {\n            vector<float> tmp;\n            auto coordname = coordname_[i].c_str();\n            check(cg_coord_read(fp, ibase, izone, coordname, coordDataType_, &rangeMin, &rangeMax, tmp.data()), format(\"read %s\\n\", coordname));\n            for (auto id = 0; id < tmp.size(); ++id) data[i][id] = tmp[id];\n        }\n    }\n\n    return data;\n}\n\nvoid CGFile::loadCoordinateInfo()\n{\n    char coordname[33];\n    cgsize_t range_min = 1, range_max = zoneInfo[0];\n\n    cg_ncoords(fp, ibase, izone, &ncoords);\n    check(cg_coord_info(fp, 1, 1, 1, &coordDataType_, coordname));\n\n    vector<double> coordsDouble(range_max, 0.0);\n    for (int icoord = 1; icoord <= ncoords; ++icoord)\n    {\n        cg_coord_info(fp, ibase, izone, icoord, &coordDataType_, coordname);\n        coordname_.push_back(coordname);\n    }\n}\n\nvoid CGFile::loadCGIOInfo()\n{\n    double rootId;\n    int cgioNum;\n\n    auto getChildrenIds = [&](double parentId)\n    {\n        int numChildren, tmp;\n        vector<double> childrenIds;\n\n        cgio_number_children(cgioNum, parentId, &numChildren);\n        childrenIds.assign(numChildren, 0.0);\n        cgio_children_ids(cgioNum, parentId, 1, numChildren, &tmp, childrenIds.data());\n\n        return childrenIds;\n    };\n\n    auto lookupNodeIdByLabel = [&](double nodeId, string label)\n    {\n        char tmpLabel[CGIO_MAX_LABEL_LENGTH + 1];\n        vector<double> rst;\n\n        for (auto id : getChildrenIds(nodeId))\n        {\n            cgio_get_label(cgioNum, id, tmpLabel);\n            if (string(tmpLabel) == label) { rst.push_back(id); }\n        }\n\n        return rst;\n    };\n\n    cg_get_cgio(fp, &cgioNum);\n    cgio_get_root_id(cgioNum, &rootId);\n    auto baseId = lookupNodeIdByLabel(rootId, \"CGNSBase_t\");\n    auto zoneId = lookupNodeIdByLabel(baseId[0], \"Zone_t\");\n    auto sectionId = lookupNodeIdByLabel(zoneId[0], \"Elements_t\");\n\n    char name[CGIO_MAX_NAME_LENGTH + 1];\n    char type[CGIO_MAX_DATATYPE_LENGTH + 1];\n    for (auto id : sectionId)\n    {\n        cgio_get_name(cgioNum, id, name);\n        // sectionNodeId.insert({name, id});\n        cgio_get_data_type(cgioNum, id, type);\n        // sectionDataType.insert({name, type});\n    }\n}\n\nCGFile::Section &CGFile::loadSection(const int id)\n{\n    auto &curSection = sections_[id];\n    // if(!curSection.data.empty()) return curSection;\n    curSection.clear();\n\n    cgsize_t ID = curSection.start;\n    vector<cgsize_t> tmp;\n    auto loader = CellLoader{curSection, fp, curSection.start, curSection.end};\n    while (loader.nextCell(tmp)) { curSection.addCell(ID++, tmp, loader.flag); }\n\n    return curSection;\n}\n\nCGFile::Section &CGFile::loadSection(const int id, const cgsize_t start, const cgsize_t end)\n{\n    auto &curSection = sections_[id];\n    // if(!curSection.data.empty() && start>=curSection.start && end <= curSection.end) return curSection;\n    curSection.clear();\n    curSection.start = start;\n    curSection.end = end;\n\n    cgsize_t ID = start;\n    vector<cgsize_t> tmp;\n    auto loader = CellLoader{curSection, fp, start, end};\n    while (loader.nextCell(tmp)) { curSection.addCell(ID++, tmp, loader.flag); }\n\n    return curSection;\n}\n\nCGFile::Section CGFile::loadSection(const vector<int> idList)\n{\n    Section curS;\n    vector<cgsize_t> tmp;\n    string name;\n    set<ElementType_t> types;\n\n    for (auto bodyId : idList)\n    {\n        auto &curBody = sections_[bodyId];\n        curBody.clear();\n        cgsize_t ID = curBody.start;\n        name += (curBody.name + string(\"_\"));\n        curS.start = std::min(curS.start, curBody.start);\n        curS.end = std::max(curS.end, curBody.end);\n        types.insert(curBody.cellType);\n        auto loader = CellLoader{curBody, fp, curBody.start, curBody.end};\n        while (loader.nextCell(tmp)) { curS.addCell(ID++, tmp, loader.flag); }\n    }\n    std::memcpy(curS.name, name.c_str(), std::min((size_t)31, name.rfind('_')));\n    curS.cellType = types.size() == 1 ? *types.begin() : ElementType_t::MIXED;\n\n    return curS;\n}\n\nCGFile::Section CGFile::loadSection(const vector<int> idList, const cgsize_t start, const cgsize_t end)\n{\n    Section curS;\n    vector<cgsize_t> tmp;\n    string name;\n    set<ElementType_t> types;\n    curS.start = start;\n    curS.end = end;\n    cgsize_t ID = start;\n\n    for (auto bodyId : idList)\n    {\n        auto &curBody = sections_[bodyId];\n        if (curBody.start > end || curBody.end < start) continue;\n\n        curBody.clear();\n        name += (curBody.name + string(\"_\"));\n        types.insert(curBody.cellType);\n        auto loader = CellLoader{curBody, fp, std::max(start, curBody.start), std::min(end, curBody.end)};\n        while (loader.nextCell(tmp)) { curS.addCell(ID++, tmp, loader.flag); }\n    }\n    std::memcpy(curS.name, name.c_str(), std::min((size_t)31, name.rfind('_')));\n    curS.cellType = types.size() == 1 ? *types.begin() : ElementType_t::MIXED;\n\n    return curS;\n}\n\ncgsize_t CGFile::nCell() const { return zoneInfo[1]; }\n\nvoid CGFile::nCell(const cgsize_t ncell) { zoneInfo[1] = ncell; }\n\ncgsize_t CGFile::nNode() const { return zoneInfo[0]; }\n\nvoid CGFile::nNode(const cgsize_t nnode) { zoneInfo[0] = nnode; }\n\nint CGFile::precision()\n{\n    int rst;\n    check(cg_precision(fp, &rst));\n\n    return rst;\n}\n\nCGFile::Section &CGFile::section(const int id) { return sections_[id]; }\n\nstring CGFile::stringAFace(const vector<cgsize_t> &face)\n{\n    if (face.empty()) return \"\";\n\n    set<cgsize_t> tmp;\n    for (auto it : face) tmp.insert(it);\n\n    return std::accumulate(std::next(tmp.begin()), tmp.end(), std::to_string(*tmp.begin()), [](string sum, const cgsize_t id) { return sum + \"-\" + std::to_string(id); });\n}\n\nvoid CGFile::writeCoordinate(const vector<vector<double>> &data)\n{\n    int dummy;\n\n    // base\n    check(cg_base_write(fp, \"CGNSBASE\", 3, 3, &dummy), \"write base\\n\");\n\n    // zone\n    check(cg_zone_write(fp, 1, \"ZONE\", zoneInfo, Unstructured, &dummy), \"write zone\\n\");\n\n    // grid\n    check(cg_grid_write(fp, 1, 1, \"GridCoordinates\", &igrid), \"write grid\\n\");\n\n    // coordinate\n    for (auto iCoord : {0, 1, 2})\n    {\n        const char *coordname = coordname_[iCoord].c_str();\n        check(cg_coord_write(fp, 1, 1, RealDouble, coordname, data[iCoord].data(), &dummy), format(\"write coordinate %s\\n\", coordname));\n    }\n}\n\nvoid CGFile::writeSection(Section &curS, const map<cgsize_t, cgsize_t> &nodeIdG2L)\n{\n    // update id\n    curS.dataSize = std::accumulate(curS.data.begin(), curS.data.end(), 0, [](cgsize_t sum, vector<cgsize_t> vec) { return sum + vec.size(); });\n\n    curS.start = idOffset_;\n    curS.end = curS.start + curS.data.size() - 1;\n    idOffset_ = curS.end + 1;\n\n    int dummy;\n    if (curS.isMixed())\n    {\n        curS.dataSize += curS.typeFlag.size();\n        vector<cgsize_t> data(curS.dataSize, 0);\n        int icell = 0, i = 0;\n        for (auto it : curS.data)\n        {\n            data[i++] = curS.typeFlag[icell++];\n            for (auto j : it) { data[i++] = nodeIdG2L.at(j); }\n        }\n        check(cg_poly_section_write(fp, 1, 1, curS.name, curS.cellType, curS.start, curS.end, 0, data.data(), curS.offset.data(), &dummy), format(\"file %s write section %s\\n\", filename_.c_str(), curS.name));\n    }\n    else\n    {\n        vector<cgsize_t> data(curS.dataSize);\n        int i = 0;\n        for (auto it : curS.data)\n        {\n            for (auto j : it) { data[i++] = nodeIdG2L.at(j); }\n        }\n        check(cg_section_write(fp, 1, 1, curS.name, curS.cellType, curS.start, curS.end, curS.nBdy, data.data(), &dummy), format(\"file %s write section %s\\n\", filename_.c_str(), curS.name));\n    }\n\n    std::cout << format(\"  %s: write section %s [%d: %d, %d]\\n\", filename_.c_str(), curS.name, curS.end - curS.start + 1, curS.start, curS.end);\n}\n\nvoid CGFile::writeGlobalInfo(const Section &curS, const cgsize_t n, const cgsize_t start, const cgsize_t end)\n{\n    cg_goto(fp, 1, \"Zone_t\", 1, curS.name, 0, \"end\");\n    std::ostringstream ostr;\n    ostr << \"GlobalNumber:\" << n << '\\n';\n    ostr << \"GlobalStart:\" << start << '\\n';\n    ostr << \"GlobalEnd:\" << end << '\\n';\n    auto str = ostr.str();\n    cg_descriptor_write(\"GlobalDomainInfo\", str.c_str());\n}\n\n} // namespace MeshCut\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1772620023811}}
{"type":"message","id":"e321528f","parentId":"d1117884","timestamp":"2026-03-04T10:27:21.569Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the code. Let me compile the research report.\n\n**Key Findings from Code Analysis:**\n\n1. **distribute() Function Analysis** (metisCutter.cpp, lines 15-26):\n   - Simple round-robin distribution based on rank\n   - No consideration of cell complexity or load weight\n   - Each rank gets `len/n` cells + possible 1 extra\n   - Problem: Doesn't account for actual computational load per cell\n\n2. **collect_interface() Function Analysis** (metisCutter.cpp, lines 60-126):\n   - Already uses point-to-point communication (improved from original all-to-all)\n   - But still has O(SIZE) communication pattern per rank\n   - Uses string serialization for interface data\n   - Packing/unpacking involves string operations (stringSplit)\n\n3. **Interface String Matching Analysis**:\n   - Face matching uses `hashFace()` function (metisCutter.h)\n   - Hash is a simple XOR-based hash of node IDs\n   - `outerFace_` is `unordered_map<int, unordered_map<size_t, vector<cgsize_t>>>`\n   - The hash is `size_t` (integer) - actually already using integer hash!\n   - String operations in `collect_interface()` are for serialization, not matching\n\nLet me write the research report now.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"现在我已经完成了代码分析和资料搜索，开始撰写调研报告。"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"tool-18014d410706490a9de3307eeaafd23c","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"mkdir -p $HOME/.openclaw-reports/search-agent/"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bailian","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":6646,"output":593,"cacheRead":44863,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":52102,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1772620023814}}
